# Editors: Zed and aider

Two tools, deliberately. They are good at different halves of the work and both
address roles rather than model names, so neither needs touching when a model
changes.

| | aider | Zed |
|---|---|---|
| Where | terminal | editor |
| Unit of work | a diff you approve | inline edits and agent runs |
| Best for | changes you want to review before they land | exploring, refactoring in place |
| Agent | n/a — it always shows you the diff | yes, with restrictable tools |

---

## aider

### Config

```yaml
# ~/.aider.conf.yml
# Roles, not model names. OPENAI_API_BASE + OPENAI_API_KEY come from
# scripts/aliases.zsh per invocation. One local model (ADR 0016, D1).
model: openai/local-main
weak-model: openai/local-main
dark-mode: true
auto-commits: true
map-tokens: 4096
```

`weak-model` is used for commit messages and summaries. With one resident
model there is nothing cheaper to point it at, and nothing to evict.
`ask-cloud` overrides both with the Gemini id from `config/roles.tsv`, so a
cloud session never sends its commit messages anywhere else.

### Architect mode

aider can split planning from editing. With one local model both sides are
`local-main`; the value left is that the design step stays visible.

```bash
ask-arch    # aider --architect --model openai/local-main --editor-model openai/local-main
```

`ask-deep` runs aider on `local-deep`, a slower, more careful model, loaded
beside `local-main` for that session (ADR 0020, 0028). It is also in Zed's
model picker, labelled "load first": a request to an unloaded role can be
answered by whatever is loaded, without saying so. To use it from Zed, run
`scripts/demand-load.sh local-deep up` on the host, and
`scripts/demand-load.sh local-deep down` when done. `local-main` stays loaded.

### Switching mid-session

```
/model openai/local-main       the only local role
                              (cloud: quit and start `ask-cloud` instead —
                               it uses its own key and endpoint)
/ask                          question without editing anything
/architect                    toggle architect mode
```

`/ask` is worth the habit — it answers without proposing a diff, which keeps you
deciding what to change.

### Deliberate review

You have `auto-commits: true`, which is convenient and also the setting most
likely to let a change land without you reading it. For anything touching
flagged sensitive code:

```bash
aider --no-auto-commits
```

---

## Zed

### Point it at oMLX

Directly in `~/.config/zed/settings.json` (ADR 0016). Every model slot —
agent, inline assistant, commit message, thread summary, compaction,
subagent — is `hearth/local-main`, so nothing ever asks for a second model.
**No cloud provider on this host (D3)**: cloud is `ask-cloud` in a terminal.

```json
{
  "agent": {
    "default_model":          { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-main" },
    "inline_assistant_model": { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-main" },
    "commit_message_model":   { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-main" },
    "thread_summary_model":   { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-main" },
    "compaction_model":       { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-main" },
    "subagent_model":         { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-main" },
    "auto_compact":           { "enabled": true, "threshold": 110000 }
  },
  "language_models": {
    "openai_compatible": {
      "hearth": {
        "api_url": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1",
        "available_models": [
          { "name": "local-main", "display_name": "<written by scripts/zed-sync.sh>", "max_tokens": 131072 }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Automatic compaction runs at 110,000 tokens. The loaded window is 131,072.
Past that the server refuses the prompt, and Zed compacts on that error.
110,000 leaves about 21,000 tokens for the turn that crosses the line and
for Zed's count running behind the server. Zed's default of 90% (about
118,000) sits inside that lag. Compaction summarizes the older turns, sends
that summary plus the recent turns, and leaves the original thread in the
panel under **Context Compacted**. `/compact` does the same by hand.
**New From Summary** starts a fresh thread from it. The summary replaces the
cached prefix, so the next turn reads the new thread from the start.

`name` is the role and is what Zed sends. `display_name` and `max_tokens`
are generated by `scripts/zed-sync.sh` from `config/roles.tsv`: the label is
`role — <weights-leaf>` (e.g. `local-main — Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MLX-4bit`) so the
picker shows the backing model while the request still uses the role
(ADR 0034), and `max_tokens` matches the role's context. Preflight warns if
they drift. Add a new role's entry by hand once; the sync keeps it current.

The token goes in through the UI (Agent panel → settings → `hearth` → API
key) so it lands in the keychain rather than a dotfile. Use the host token on
this machine; each laptop gets its own (phase 4).

### MCP example: auslaw

`auslaw` is not installed. `docs/MCP.md` uses its former setup as a worked
example: a reviewed stdio child, no jade cookie, seven AustLII tools exposed
only in a Research profile, and confirmation before each call.

Zed's built-in **Write** profile sets `enable_all_context_servers: true`
(Zed 1.26 defaults), which would expose any configured server in an ordinary
thread. Settings must redefine Write with that set to `false`. A profile
override **replaces** the built-in profile (`name` is required, tools are not
merged), so the override must carry the full intended tool list. Re-check
Write and Ask after a Zed upgrade: a partial override can fail the whole
settings load. `tool_permissions.default` is `confirm`, so each MCP call asks
first.

If auslaw is restored, switching to Research is the act of saying "this query
goes to AustLII." Follow the prerequisites, UI fields, JSON profile and checks
in `docs/MCP.md`; do not infer a command from this document.

Write and Ask set `fetch` and `search_web` to false. Zed's built-in Ask
profile includes both, so Ask is overridden here the same way Write is.
Search is the Web profile, or Write + search when the thread also edits.

### MCP: searxng

`searxng` is a `context_servers` stdio entry for the pinned SearXNG MCP
server (`docs/MCP.md`, ADR 0022, 0029). Its search tool sends the query text
through the host's SearXNG to public engines. Only `searxng_web_search` is
on. `web_url_read` (any URL) and the other two tools stay off. Each tool
call asks first.

**Web** has an empty built-in tool map and `default_model` `hearth/local-main`.
A search result cannot read a file.

**Write + search** (`write-web`) has Write's tools and no pinned model, so
the thread stays on `local-main` or `local-deep`. It can read files and put
them into the query. It is not the default profile.

Switching to either profile is the act of saying "this query goes to the
search engines". Write + search can read a file and put personal information
from it into that query.

### On a laptop

Through an SSH tunnel to the host — `docs/LAPTOP.md`. Zed points at
`http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` exactly as on the host (the tunnel's local end),
with the **laptop's own** token. Run `ai-tunnel` before opening Zed. A Gemini
provider is allowed on the laptop (D3). Web stays on `hearth/local-main`.
Write + search has no pinned model. Do not switch either profile to Gemini.

Zed's SSH remote projects also work: edit files on the host over SSH while
the model call goes through the tunnel. That needs a normal SSH key, not the
tunnel-only one.

### Thinking

Thinking for `local-main` is set on the server by `scripts/omlx.sh`
(`docs/MODELS.md`), and healthcheck fails without it. Leave
`enable_thinking` out of `agent.default_model`. In Zed 1.27 an
`openai_compatible` model supports thinking only if its `available_models`
entry sets `reasoning_effort`; without that, `enable_thinking` is forced off
and no reasoning field is sent, so the server default applies (read from
`crates/language_models/src/provider/open_ai_compatible.rs` at v1.27.0).
Adding `reasoning_effort` would change that: `enable_thinking: false` would
then send effort `none`. Do not add it.

### Edit prediction

Three choices. Default is off. Host oMLX does not serve edit prediction
(ADR 0033 amend: the 2026-11-07 paired gate failed).

**1. Off (default).** Safe for every buffer. Zed 1.27 key:

```json
{ "edit_predictions": { "provider": "none" } }
```

On the Free plan, `"provider": "zed"` still posts to Zed and gets 403 — the
request has already left (this laptop's Zed log, 2026-10-31). Keep `none`
until you pick an option below.

**2. Laptop `local-edit` (local).** Build weights
(`docs/MODELS.md` → *Building `local-edit`*), then
`client/edit-model.sh laptop-up` and `client/edit-model.sh bench`. Point Zed
at the laptop's own oMLX (port 8000 by default):

```json
"edit_predictions": {
  "provider": "open_ai_compatible_api",
  "open_ai_compatible_api": {
    "api_url": "http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/completions",
    "model": "local-edit",
    "prompt_format": "sweep",
    "max_output_tokens": 512
  }
}
```

The key goes in through the UI (status bar edit-prediction menu → Configure
Providers → OpenAI-compatible API key), so it lands in the keychain — use the
laptop oMLX's own key. Works with the tunnel down. Do not point this at the
host through the tunnel: host `:1234` does not load `local-edit`, and when it
did, agent c3 starved predictions.

**3. Paid Zed Zeta (cloud).** With a paid Zed plan, `"provider": "zed"` uses
Zed's hosted Zeta. Cursor context leaves the machine. Allowed only for
buffers that `docs/ROUTING.md` would send to cloud — never for sensitive,
PII, credentials, or flagged repos (rule 3). `scripts/zed-sync.sh --check`
(and preflight) still report DRIFT for `"zed"` on purpose: enabling it is
an operator override, not a green path. Do not add Zeta (or any other cloud
model) as a host Zed language-model provider.

`client/edit-model.sh` is the one script a laptop runs for option 2: it
writes only `~/.hearth/models` and the laptop's own oMLX.

### Sandbox (Zed 1.14)

Since Zed 1.14 (August 2026) the agent panel's **terminal and fetch tools**
run sandboxed by default: no writes outside the project or to `.git`, no
network. It does **not** cover the edit tool, MCP servers (so not
`searxng`), ACP agents, the normal terminal, or LSP servers. It complements
`tool_permissions`; it does not replace `scripts/sandboxed.sh` for aider.

`agent.sandbox_permissions.network_hosts` widens it. Every host listed
there is an egress path for an injected agent (`docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`,
accepted risk 1). Keep it empty on the host; on a laptop, list only what a
thread needs and say why.

### External agents (ACP)

Zed can run other vendors' agents (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI and others)
over the Agent Client Protocol, installed from the ACP Registry. None of the
controls above apply to them: profiles and `tool_permissions` govern only
Zed's own agent, the sandbox does not cover them, and they call their own
cloud with their own keys. Zed may also forward its MCP servers to them.

**No ACP agent on the host.** On a laptop one is a cloud tool like
`ask-cloud`: non-sensitive repos only.

### Agent profiles — the reason Zed is here

Zed's agent has **profiles with configurable tool access**. You can define one
that reads and proposes but cannot apply edits or run commands, and a separate
one that can.

That is the closest thing shipping today to an assistant that helps without
taking over, and it is why Zed earns its place here. JetBrains now runs its
agent on local models too (see *Other editors*), but Zed's profiles are the
finer-grained control.

Suggested split:

- **explore** — read files, search, discuss. No edits, no terminal. Use when you
  are trying to understand something.
- **build** — full tools. Use when you already know what you want.

The distinction is not about safety. It is about which mode you are in.

---

## JetBrains AI Assistant

Optional. Calls `local-main` the same way Zed does. On a laptop the tunnel
must be up (`ai-tunnel`).

1. **Host:** a key for this IDE, so it can be revoked on its own:
   `scripts/omlx.sh sub-key add jetbrains-<laptop>` (prompt does not echo).
2. **IDE:** Settings → Tools → AI Assistant → Providers & API keys →
   **OpenAI Compatible**. Base URL `http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1`. Type the key
   into the API key field; the IDE keeps it in its own password store.
   **Test Connection**.
3. **Assign the model by hand.** Models from an OpenAI-compatible endpoint
   are not assigned to features automatically. Pick `local-main` for chat
   and each feature you use. Never `local-deep` unless it is loaded.
4. **Offline mode on** for sensitive repos, and no cloud provider or JetBrains
   AI subscription model on the host (rule 3). A feature with no local model
   assigned may use JetBrains' cloud if a subscription is signed in.

Not verified against oMLX: whether Test Connection passes oMLX's token check,
and whether Junie uses a local provider from the IDE or only from Junie CLI.
One JetBrains IDE configures all of them only if settings sync is on; each
IDE otherwise needs steps 2–4.

## Xcode 27

Optional. Swift work only.

1. **Host:** `scripts/omlx.sh sub-key add xcode-<laptop>`.
2. **Xcode:** Settings → Intelligence → Add a Chat Provider → **Internet
   Hosted**. URL `http://127.0.0.1:1234` (no `/v1`: Xcode adds the path).
   Header `Authorization`; type the key, without `Bearer` (Xcode adds it).
   Model `local-main`.
3. **No Claude, Gemini or OpenAI agent** in Intelligence for sensitive repos,
   and none on the host. ACP agents added to Xcode follow
   *External agents (ACP)* above.

"Locally Hosted" does not work here: it takes only a port and sends no key,
and oMLX refuses a request without one. Not verified: whether Internet
Hosted accepts plain `http`. If it refuses, say so in this file rather than
adding a TLS proxy in front of `:1234`.

---

## Why two tools, and why this matters

The 2026 research on AI-assisted development is not comfortable reading.
Developers using AI assistance scored **17% lower on comprehension** afterwards,
with the steepest decline among those who fully delegated (Shen and Tamkin,
2026). The framing that stuck is **output up, capability down** — and the
distinction between *atrophy*, losing skills you had, and *foreclosure*, never
building them. Roughly half of programmer actions in LLM-assisted workflows show
identifiable cognitive bias, mostly anchoring on whatever the model proposed
first (Zhou et al., 2026).

Nothing ships to address this. The one paper directly on it —
[Agents That Teach](https://arxiv.org/html/2607.06101) — is a design proposal,
not a product. The measurement is years ahead of the tooling.

So the mitigations here are choices, not features:

- **aider shows a diff and waits.** That is friction, and the friction is the
  point. `/ask` and `--no-auto-commits` add more when you want it.
- **Zed's explore profile can't edit.** Reading and discussing without the option
  to apply keeps you deciding.
- **Local models are weaker, and that is quietly protective.** A model that
  cannot one-shot the answer keeps you in the loop — the opposite of how model
  quality is usually discussed.
- **Architect mode separates deciding from typing**, which keeps the design
  decision visible instead of buried in a diff.

None of this is about distrusting the output. It is about noticing when you have
stopped thinking, which by definition is not something you notice.

---

## Other editors

Not recommended here, recorded so the question does not come round again.
Checked 2026-11-07 by web search; vendor claims, not tested on this host.
Rule 3 applies to all of them: a tool that sends code to a cloud service,
for indexing or an agent, is for non-sensitive repos only.

| Editor | Local models | Cloud by default | State |
|---|---|---|---|
| JetBrains (AI Assistant, Junie) — setup above | Ollama, LM Studio, LiteLLM. Local models can be assigned to features beyond chat. Junie (out of beta June 2026, on ACP) can use them; one JetBrains answer says only from Junie CLI. "Junie Local" bundles its own local model and needs a 64 GB M5 Mac | JetBrains AI subscription or own keys | Most credible second editor for local work. Not benched against oMLX's token auth |
| Xcode 27 — setup above | "Locally Hosted" provider by port (Intelligence settings). Unverified whether it can send oMLX's token | Built-in Claude, Gemini and OpenAI agents with your own keys; ACP agents | Swift only. Locally Hosted at `:1234`, never its cloud agents, for sensitive work |
| VS Code + Copilot | Bring-your-own-model includes Ollama; still needs a GitHub login | Copilot | Works; an extension stack rather than built in |
| Cursor | Custom endpoint possible; earlier it rejected non-HTTPS base URLs | Indexing and search use Cursor's cloud | Not used here |
| Windsurf, Kiro, Antigravity | Nothing found | Agent-first cloud IDEs | Not used here |
| Cline, aider | Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint | No | aider is the second tool |
# Laptop access

**SSH tunnel only.** The host's oMLX stays
bound to `127.0.0.1:1234`; a laptop forwards a local port to it over SSH. Same
setup on the LAN and away from it, traffic encrypted, nothing on the host
exposed to the network.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph laptop["Laptop"]
        lz["Zed / aider / ai-*"]
        lp["127.0.0.1:1234<br/>127.0.0.1:3000<br/>127.0.0.1:8888"]
    end
    subgraph host["Host"]
        sshd["sshd :22<br/>tunnel-only key"]
        srv["oMLX 127.0.0.1:1234<br/>token per machine"]
        webui["Open WebUI 127.0.0.1:3000<br/>SearXNG 127.0.0.1:8888"]
    end
    gem["Gemini<br/>laptop's own key"]
    lz --> lp
    lp -->|"ssh -L"| sshd
    sshd --> srv
    sshd --> webui
    lz -.->|"ask-cloud only"| gem
```

## What each laptop gets

| Thing | Where | Why |
|---|---|---|
| API key `laptop-<name>` | laptop `~/.hearth-key` (mode 600) | revoke one machine without touching the others |
| `local-edit` on the laptop's own oMLX (optional) | `~/.hearth/models`, linked into `~/.omlx/models` by `client/edit-model.sh laptop-up` | local edit prediction; host does not serve it. Paid Zed Zeta is the cloud alternative (`docs/IDES.md`, ADR 0033) |
| API keys `jetbrains-<name>`, `xcode-<name>` (optional) | typed into the IDE's own key field | revoke one editor without touching the laptop's key (`docs/IDES.md`) |
| SSH key `hearth_tunnel` | laptop `~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel`; host `authorized_keys` with `command="/usr/bin/false",restrict,port-forwarding,permitopen=…,permitlisten="none:1"` | the key can `-L` to `127.0.0.1:1234`, `:3000` and `:8888` only — no shell, no commands, no `-R`, no other ports |
| Google API key (optional) | laptop `~/.hearth-gemini-key` (mode 600) | `ask-cloud` with the laptop's own key; the host's key never leaves |
| `client/hearth-client.sh` / `.ps1` | laptop `~/.hearth-client.sh` | `ai-tunnel`, `ai-ping`, `ai-models`, `ai-ask`, `ai-webui`, `ask*`, `ask-deep`, `ask-cloud` |

Secrets are read from files per command and never exported into the
laptop's shell. A world-readable key file is refused.

## Setup

On the host, `scripts/client-setup.sh <laptop-name>` prints every step with
this host's name and user filled in (`--windows` for PowerShell). In short:

1. **Host:** with oMLX listening (`scripts/omlx.sh status`), register a
   laptop key. The prompt does not echo. A reboot runs `scripts/omlx.sh up`,
   which keeps sub-keys already registered.
   `scripts/omlx.sh sub-key add laptop-<name>`
2. **Laptop:** `ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel`, and a `Host hearth`
   entry in `~/.ssh/config` pointing at the host with `IdentitiesOnly yes`.
3. **Host:** `scripts/client-setup.sh <name> --authorize hearth_tunnel.pub`.
   It refuses a key that is already authorized without restrictions.
4. **Laptop:** copy `client/hearth-client.sh` to `~/.hearth-client.sh`,
   store the token and (optionally) a Google key, add
   `HEARTH_SSH=hearth`, `HEARTH_CLOUD_MODEL=…` and `source ~/.hearth-client.sh`
   to the shell rc.
5. **Laptop:** `ai-tunnel && ai-ping && ai-models`.

`ai-ping` answers: `up`, `BAD TOKEN` (revoked or wrong token), `NO ANSWER`
(tunnel up but host asleep or oMLX down — on the host, `scripts/boot.sh`),
or `DOWN` (no tunnel — `ai-tunnel`).
If something else is already listening on `127.0.0.1:1234`, the client refuses and does not send the token.

## AI-assisted setup

For an AI assistant (Claude Code, Cursor, …) running **on the laptop** in a
clone of this repo. The assistant is probably a cloud model: anything it reads
or prints goes to its vendor. That decides every rule below.

**Never:** read, print, grep, copy or edit `~/.hearth-key`,
`~/.hearth-gemini-key` or `~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel` (the private key); ask the
person to paste a token or key into the chat; run anything in `scripts/`
(host-only); add a router port-forward; suggest turning on Serve on Local
Network; configure hearth as a Cursor model provider (see below). Checking a
secret file's permissions with `ls -l` is fine.

**1. Ask the person for:**

- the host as `user@host.local` (on the host: `id -un` and
  `scutil --get LocalHostName`);
- the laptop's name for its token and key comment (letters, digits, `-`, `_`);
- shell: macOS / Linux (zsh or bash) or Windows PowerShell.

Read the Gemini model id yourself: the `cloud-cheap` row of
`config/roles.tsv`, column 3. Do not copy it anywhere except the
`HEARTH_CLOUD_MODEL` line.

**2. You may run** (macOS / Linux; Windows equivalents are in
`client/hearth-client.ps1`'s header):

```bash
# Interactive (passphrase prompt) — ask the person to run it, or run it if your
# terminal is interactive:
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel -C hearth-tunnel-<name>

# ~/.ssh/config — append only if there is no "Host hearth" entry already
printf 'Host hearth\n  HostName %s\n  User %s\n  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel\n  IdentitiesOnly yes\n' <host.local> <user> >> ~/.ssh/config

cp client/hearth-client.sh ~/.hearth-client.sh
printf 'export HEARTH_SSH=hearth\nexport HEARTH_CLOUD_MODEL=%s\nsource ~/.hearth-client.sh\n' <cloud-cheap id> >> ~/.zshrc   # or ~/.bashrc
```

Showing the person `~/.ssh/hearth_tunnel.pub` is fine — it is public.

**3. Stop and hand over to the person** for each of these, then wait:

1. *On the host*, while oMLX is listening, the person runs
   `scripts/omlx.sh sub-key add laptop-<name>` and types the token.
   Do not ask them to paste it into the chat.
2. *On the host*, with the `.pub` file copied over:
   `scripts/client-setup.sh <name> --authorize hearth_tunnel.pub`.
3. *On the laptop, in their own terminal* (not through you), store the secrets:

   ```bash
   umask 077
   read -rs t && printf '%s' "$t" > ~/.hearth-key && unset t          # laptop-<name> token
   read -rs t && printf '%s' "$t" > ~/.hearth-gemini-key && unset t   # optional: this laptop's OWN Google key
   ```

Then confirm with `ls -l ~/.hearth-key ~/.hearth-gemini-key`: mode
`-rw-------`, non-zero size. Nothing more.

**4. Prove it** in a new shell. Both halves are required: the first shows the
key works, the second that it can do nothing else.

| Command | Expect |
|---|---|
| `ai-tunnel` | `tunnel: up` (the first run asks the person to confirm the host fingerprint) |
| `ai-ping` | `up` |
| `ai-models` | `local-main` (plus `local-deep` only while the host has it loaded) |
| `ai-ask "say ok"` | a short reply from local-main |
| `ssh hearth true; echo $?` | **non-zero**, no shell: the key is tunnel-only |
| `ask-cloud` (optional) | aider on Gemini with the laptop's own key |
| `ask-deep` (optional) | refuses with "not loaded on the host" unless the host ran `scripts/demand-load.sh local-deep up`; the laptop cannot load it (tunnel-only key). `local-main` stays loaded beside it |

If `ai-ping` says `BAD TOKEN`, the person re-runs the `read -rs` step with
the right token; `NO ANSWER` means the host is asleep or oMLX is down
(fix on the host); `DOWN` means no tunnel. If `ssh hearth true` **succeeds**,
stop: the key was authorized without restrictions — tell the person, and do
not continue.

**5. Editors.** Zed: the block in [Zed on the laptop](#zed-on-the-laptop),
with the laptop's token entered through Zed's UI by the person.

**Cursor cannot use hearth as its model provider.** Cursor's backend makes
the model call, not the laptop, so a `localhost` base URL never reaches the
tunnel; the only workaround is a public endpoint, which the *Never* list
forbids, and prompts would still pass through Cursor's servers (checked
2026-10-31). Use Zed, aider or `ask` for local-main on the laptop; Cursor
with its own models only, and not on sensitive repos.

## Zed on the laptop

Same provider as the host, through the tunnel:

```json
{
  "language_models": {
    "openai_compatible": {
      "hearth": {
        "api_url": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1",
        "available_models": [
          { "name": "local-main", "display_name": "local-main", "max_tokens": 131072 }
        ]
      }
    }
  },
  "agent": { "default_model": { "provider": "hearth", "model": "local-main" } }
}
```

Token through the UI (Agent panel → settings → `hearth` → API key): the
**laptop's** token, not the host's. Start `ai-tunnel` before Zed, or Zed's
models show as unavailable.

A Gemini provider **is allowed on the laptop** (D3), with the laptop's own
key. The host has none. Keep sensitive repos on `hearth/local-main`.

If using the legal-research worked example from `docs/MCP.md`, install and
review its source on the laptop too; its stdio process runs beside Zed, not on
the host. The same "Write enables every server" trap applies.

## Open WebUI from the laptop

`ai-webui` opens `http://127.0.0.1:3000` through the tunnel — the host's own
instance, no login, same history. Nothing to install on the laptop.

## Web search in the laptop's Zed (optional)

The tunnel also forwards the host's SearXNG to `127.0.0.1:8888` (ADR 0022;
`HEARTH_SEARXNG_PORT` changes the laptop end, `0` leaves it out). To give the
laptop's Zed agent web search, install the same pinned MCP server there and
register it the way the host does (`docs/MCP.md`, `docs/IDES.md`):

```bash
npm install --ignore-scripts --save-exact --prefix ~/.local/share/hearth/mcp-searxng mcp-searxng@2.9.0
```

Then a `searxng` stdio context server in Zed with
`SEARXNG_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8888` (or your `HEARTH_SEARXNG_PORT`). Enable
`searxng_web_search` only, in `web` (no file tools, `default_model`
`hearth/local-main`) and, for a coding thread, `write-web` (Write's tools,
no pinned model). Do not switch either profile to Gemini. Run `ai-tunnel`
before searching. Search queries go to public search engines via the host.
An agent that can read files can put personal information from them into
that query.

SearXNG has no login. Once the tunnel is up, any process on the laptop can
POST a query to `127.0.0.1:8888` and the host will send it to the public
engines.

**Existing laptops:** the host's tunnel keys were widened with
`scripts/client-setup.sh --update-keys` (2026-11-01). On the laptop, copy the
new client (`scripts/client-setup.sh <name> --install user@laptop` from the
host), then `ai-tunnel-stop && ai-tunnel`. `ai-tunnel` refuses to report the
tunnel up when `:1234` is open and `:3000` or `:8888` is not, and when `:1234`
is open but the listener is not this client's ssh.

## Revoking a laptop

1. While oMLX is up: `scripts/omlx.sh sub-key revoke laptop-<name>`.
2. Host: delete the `hearth-tunnel-<name>` line from `~/.ssh/authorized_keys`.

Removing the tunnel key cuts the path. Revoke the sub-key so a reboot does
not let it back in.

## Never

- Port-forward `:22`, `:1234`, `:3000` or `:8888` on the router.
- Turn on Serve on Local Network for a laptop — the tunnel makes it unnecessary,
  and it would put the token on the LAN in plain HTTP.
- Copy the host's token or Google key to a laptop.

## Host SSH hardening (recommended, not done for you)

macOS Remote Login accepts passwords by default. With keys in place:

```bash
echo 'PasswordAuthentication no
KbdInteractiveAuthentication no' | sudo tee /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/100-hearth.conf
sudo launchctl kickstart -k system/com.openssh.sshd
```

Test a key login from the laptop **before** closing your current session.
# MCP registry

Server → tools → which clients may reach them. This file and the client config
docs (`docs/IDES.md`) are the only places a concrete MCP server may be named.
Update both together, plus an ADR. Since ADR 0016 servers live in the client
(Zed `context_servers`), not the gateway (ADR 0008, superseded).

MCP tools are a **second egress path**. Keeping cloud models off `:1234`
stops a prompt reaching a cloud *model*; it says nothing about a tool that
calls the internet on its own account. That is why every server below declares
where it goes, and why a networked server is enabled only in a profile named
for that egress.

## Registered

| Server | Transport | Egress | Tools | Clients |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `searxng` | stdio | local SearXNG `127.0.0.1:8888` → public search engines | 1 of 4 | host Zed, `web` and `write-web` (ADR 0022, 0029) |

## Worked example: auslaw (not installed)

`auslaw` shows how to add a networked MCP without making it ambient. It
provides Australian and NZ case-law and legislation tools through AustLII.
It was live during migration phase 3, but neither its source nor its Zed
configuration is currently on this host. The historical source location was
`~/dev/auslaw-mcp`; this repo does not contain or install it.

**stdio, not HTTP.** Its HTTP mode calls `listen(port)` with no host, so it
binds every interface with no authentication, and defaults to `:3000` — which
is Open WebUI. stdio spawns a child process with no listener at all.

### Tools, by where they go

| Tool | Reaches | Exposed |
|---|---|---|
| `format_citation` | nothing — pure AGLC4 string formatting | yes |
| `search_legislation` | AustLII | yes |
| `search_by_citation` | AustLII | yes |
| `fetch_document_text` | AustLII | yes |
| `generate_pinpoint` | AustLII | yes |
| `validate_citation` | AustLII | yes |
| `search_cases` | AustLII (+ jade.io, disabled) | yes |
| `resolve_jade_article` | jade.io | **no** |
| `jade_citation_lookup` | jade.io | **no** |
| `search_citing_cases` | jade.io | **no** |

The worked Zed profile below exposes only the seven AustLII tools.
`JADE_SESSION_COOKIE` is deliberately absent from the child process's
environment, so the credential to use the three jade.io tools is not present.

Why exclude them: jade.io access is a reverse-engineered GWT-RPC endpoint
driven by a session cookie lifted from a browser. That is a credential in a
config file and a fragile contract with a commercial provider. AustLII is free,
public, and has neither problem. `search_cases` stays because its jade half
returns an empty array without the cookie while its AustLII half still works.

### Zed setup

Prerequisite: obtain the source from its owner, review it again, install its
locked dependencies, build it, and confirm that an absolute
`.../auslaw-mcp/dist/index.js` exists. Do not copy the historical path: it
belonged to another home directory and no longer exists here.

1. Open **Settings → AI → MCP Servers → Add Server → Add Local Server**.
2. Enter:
   - Server Name: `auslaw`
   - Command: the absolute path to the reviewed Node executable
   - Arguments: the absolute path to `auslaw-mcp/dist/index.js`
   - Environment: `MCP_TRANSPORT=stdio`
   - Timeout: `60`
3. Do **not** add `JADE_SESSION_COOKIE`. Save, then require a green
   **Server is active** indicator. A wrong command or argument exits
   immediately and otherwise looks like an empty tool list.
4. Open `zed: open settings file`. The server entry should have this shape,
   with real absolute paths:

```json
{
  "context_servers": {
    "auslaw": {
      "command": "/absolute/path/to/node",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/auslaw-mcp/dist/index.js"],
      "env": {
        "MCP_TRANSPORT": "stdio"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

5. Add a profile under `agent.profiles` which has no built-in tools and
   exposes only the seven AustLII tools:

```json
{
  "agent": {
    "profiles": {
      "research": {
        "name": "Research",
        "tools": {},
        "enable_all_context_servers": false,
        "context_servers": {
          "auslaw": {
            "tools": {
              "search_cases": true,
              "search_legislation": true,
              "search_by_citation": true,
              "fetch_document_text": true,
              "generate_pinpoint": true,
              "validate_citation": true,
              "format_citation": true
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "tool_permissions": {
      "default": "confirm"
    }
  }
}
```

6. Ensure the ordinary Write and Ask profiles set
   `enable_all_context_servers` to `false`; profile overrides replace rather
   than merge with Zed's built-ins. Reload Zed, select **Research**, and check
   that exactly those seven tools appear. Write and Ask must show none.

Switching to Research is the deliberate act of saying that the query may go
to AustLII. Keep the profile on `hearth/local-main`; host Zed has no cloud
provider. The query can carry personal information the agent has just read.
Send it only when that is intended.

## searxng

Web search for the Zed agent through the host's own SearXNG (ADR 0022).
Package `mcp-searxng` 2.9.0 (`ihor-sokoliuk/mcp-searxng`, MIT, npm with SLSA
provenance, no install scripts), installed pinned with `npm install
--ignore-scripts --save-exact` into `~/.local/share/hearth/mcp-searxng`, run by
`/opt/homebrew/bin/node` (needs Node ≥ 22). Never `npx`: that would fetch
whatever is newest on every start. Its lockfile is monitored by Snyk
(`scripts/snyk-monitor.sh`, project `hearth/mcp-searxng`).

**stdio.** It only listens if `MCP_HTTP_PORT` is set, which Zed's entry does
not. Env: `SEARXNG_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8888`, `SEARXNG_LITE_TOOLS=true`
(short schemas for a local model), `SEARXNG_MAX_RESULTS=10`.

### Tools, by where they go

| Tool | Reaches | Exposed |
|---|---|---|
| `searxng_web_search` | local SearXNG → Google, Bing and others: **the query text leaves** | yes |
| `searxng_search_suggestions` | local SearXNG → engines' autocomplete | **no** |
| `searxng_instance_info` | local SearXNG `/config` only | **no** (not needed) |
| `web_url_read` | **any public URL** | **no** |

The server has no allow-list: the exclusions are Zed profile settings, not
server-side. `web_url_read` refuses localhost, RFC1918,
link-local and private IPv6, including names that resolve to them — checked
2026-11-01 against `127.0.0.1:1234` and `localhost:8888`, both refused. Kept
off anyway: an agent that can fetch any page is both an exfiltration channel
and a prompt-injection feed.

### Who may reach it

Host Zed, two profiles (ADR 0022, 0029). **Web** has an empty built-in tool
map and `default_model` `hearth/local-main`, so a search result cannot read
a file. **Write + search** (`write-web`) has Write's tools and no pinned
model, so a coding thread on `local-main` or `local-deep` can search. The
query text still leaves, and that profile can put file contents into the
query. `confirm` on each tool call is the other gate. Write and Ask have
neither. An agent on Write + search can exfiltrate personal information by
searching with text it read from a file. See `docs/ROUTING.md`.

Laptops may run the same pinned server against the host's SearXNG through
the tunnel (`127.0.0.1:8888` on the laptop; tunnel keys allow `:8888` since
2026-11-01). Same two profiles. Do not switch either to Gemini. SearXNG has
no authentication, so any process on the laptop can send queries through the
host once the tunnel is up — `docs/LAPTOP.md`.

## Rules these enforce

**Off by default.** Zed's built-in Write profile enables every context server
(`enable_all_context_servers: true` in Zed 1.26); host settings redefine
Write with it `false`. SearXNG is on in `web` and `write-web` only.

**No cloud model alongside a networked tool.** Tool output re-enters the
prompt. Host Zed has no cloud provider (D3). Web stays on `hearth/local-main`.
Write + search sets no model, so the thread stays on `local-main` or
`local-deep`. A laptop may have a Gemini provider for other work; neither
search profile is switched to it.

**Ask before each call.** `agent.tool_permissions.default` is `confirm`.

## Checks

There is no automated MCP scope audit since the gateway went (ADR 0016).
By hand, after any Zed upgrade or settings change:

| Check | Proves |
|---|---|
| Agent panel → Write profile shows no searxng tools | the default profile cannot search via SearXNG |
| Web profile shows `searxng_web_search` and not `web_url_read`, and no file tools | a search-only thread cannot read the repo, and the URL reader stays off |
| Write + search shows `searxng_web_search`, file and edit tools, and not `web_url_read` | a coding thread can search, and the URL reader stays off |
| Write and Ask show `fetch` and `search_web` off | the built-in web tools stay off |

If following the auslaw worked example, additionally check that Write and Ask
show no auslaw tools, Research shows exactly the seven listed tools, and
`grep -c JADE ~/.config/zed/settings.json` returns `0`.

## Adding a server

1. Read its source and establish what it talks to. That is the whole job; the
   rest is typing.
2. Add it to Zed `context_servers` (stdio preferred — no listener).
3. Enable its tools only in a profile named for its egress; never in Write.
4. Add a row to this file, a note in `docs/IDES.md`, and an ADR.

### Traps

**A wrong path is silent.** A stdio server whose command or script path is
wrong exits immediately and shows no tools — identical in the UI to a
correctly scoped profile. After adding one, confirm its tools appear in the
profile that should have them.

**Zed profile overrides replace, they do not merge.** Overriding a built-in
profile needs `name` and the full tool list, or the whole settings file fails
to load (`missing field name`).
# Migration: LiteLLM + Ollama → LM Studio direct

**Status:** in progress — ADR 0016 accepted 2026-10-31.
**ADR:** `docs/decisions/0016-lmstudio-direct.md`
**Drafted:** 2026-10-31

## How to use this file (for Cursor, Claude Code, Zed, aider)

- Follow `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md`. Plan → operator refines → operator approves
  → build. **One phase at a time.** Do not start a phase until the operator says
  so in the conversation; do not start the next phase until the current gate
  passes and the operator confirms.
- **Stop at every `DECISION` and ask.** Do not pick for the operator.
- Every phase ends with a gate. Record gate output in
  `bench/results/<date>-<phase>/report.md` and tick the phase in the status
  table below in the same commit.
- LM Studio CLI flags below are written from documentation, not tested on this
  Mac. **Confirm each flag with `lms <cmd> --help` before using it**, and
  correct this file if it is wrong.
- Shell rules still apply: bash 3.2, LF only, no `declare -A` / `mapfile` /
  `${x^^}`. Preflight scans for these.
- No secrets in git. Tokens go in `~/.config/hearth/env` (host) and
  `~/.hearth-key` (clients), never in the repo.

## Status

| Phase | What | State |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | Baseline concurrency bench on the current stack | done 2026-10-31 — `bench/results/2026-10-31-phase0/report.md` |
| 1 | LM Studio beside the current stack, localhost only | done 2026-10-31: accepted by operator; wrong-tool picks (3/40) tracked as a metric, not a blocker; no GGUF bench — `bench/results/2026-10-31-phase1/report.md` |
| 2 | GPU wired-limit headroom | skipped 2026-10-31: peak 29.7 GB, memory is not the bottleneck (operator) |
| 3 | Cut host clients over (Zed, aider, WebUI, sandbox, MCP) | done 2026-10-31 — `bench/results/2026-10-31-phase3/report.md` |
| 4 | LAN access with tokens; laptop clients | host side done 2026-10-31 (SSH tunnel, D8); tunnel tested end to end on the host; **gate pending on the laptop** |
| 5 | Stop the old stack; rewrite scripts and docs | done 2026-10-31 — reboot gate passed, `bench/results/2026-10-31-phase5/report.md` |
| 6 | Soak one week, then delete old weights and services | uninstall done 2026-10-31 **without a soak** (operator); backups in `~/hearth-backups/`; ADR 0016 not yet marked accepted-complete |

## Decisions the operator must make

| ID | Question | Recommendation | Answer |
|---|---|---|---|
| D1 | Roles after migration: keep `local-code` and `local-chat` as separate models, or collapse to `local-big` + `local-embed`? | **Collapse.** Two heavy models cannot both stay resident with 3 × 64K slots inside the GPU limit, and evictions are failure F2. Keep the names as client-side aliases that resolve to `local-big` | **Collapse** (2026-10-31) |
| D2 | Slots and context for `local-big` | 3 slots × 64K. Lower to 2 if the phase 1 footprint exceeds the limit | **3 × 64K** (2026-10-31); context raised to 128K by ADR 0017. Idle footprint after that reload was 20.7 GB, so slots stay 3 |
| D3 | Does Zed **on the host** get a Gemini provider? | No on the host (keeps sensitive work local by default); yes on the laptop | **No on host** (2026-10-31) |
| D4 | Digest drift detection | Keep: hash the model directory in `config/roles.tsv` and check it in preflight | **Hash the weights** (2026-10-31): `weights` column in roles.tsv, fast check in preflight, `--deep` re-hash |
| D5 | Keep Open WebUI? | Yes, pointed at `:1234`. It no longer needs the gateway's Postgres | **Keep** (2026-10-31) |
| D6 | Headless runner: LM Studio app with "run server on login", or `llmster`? | `llmster` if it installs cleanly; the app otherwise | **The app** (changed 2026-10-31). Was `llmster`; it worked and honoured the app-set auth, but the app and llmster cannot share `~/.lmstudio` and the operator wants the GUI. The app runs the server with "Enable Local LLM Service (headless)" on; ~1.1 GB extra for its GUI processes. Removing Ollama.app: done (operator) |
| D8 | Phase 4: LAN serving or SSH tunnel? | Tunnel: nothing exposed, encrypted, same on/off LAN | **SSH tunnel only** (2026-10-31). Per-laptop token + `restrict`ed tunnel-only key |
| D7 | Soak length before phase 6 cleanup | — | **No soak** — operator deleted the old stack 2026-10-31 |

## Target

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph host["Mac Studio"]
        zed["Zed agent"]
        aider["aider / ask-*"]
        webui["Open WebUI"]
        sbx["sandboxed.sh"]
        lms["LM Studio :1234<br/>local-big, local-embed"]
    end
    laptop["LAN laptop"]
    cloud["Gemini"]
    zed --> lms
    aider --> lms
    webui --> lms
    sbx --> lms
    laptop -->|"token"| lms
    aider -.->|"ask-cloud"| cloud
```

---

## Phase 0 — Baseline

Purpose: numbers to beat, measured the same way before and after.

1. Write `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh` (bash wrapper) + `bench/concurrency.py`
   (stdlib only, `python3 -I`).
   - Args: `--base-url`, `--token-env NAME`, `--model ROLE`, `--concurrency N`
     (default 3), `--rounds R` (default 10), `--prompt-tokens T` (default
     30000).
   - Each request: a realistic agent turn — system prompt + 6 tool schemas
     (`read_file`, `write_file`, `edit_file`, `list_directory`, `grep`,
     `run_command`) + filler file content to reach T tokens, `stream: true`,
     asking for a `read_file` tool call.
   - Record per request: time to first byte, time to first token, total time,
     HTTP status, whether a valid tool call came back, finish reason.
   - Client-side timeout 180 s. A timeout is a recorded failure, not a crash.
   - Sample the inference process footprint every 2 s
     (`footprint -p <pid>` or `vmmap --summary`) and record the peak.
   - Output `raw.tsv` + `report.md` (p50/p95 TTFT, failures, peak footprint).
2. Run against the current stack: `--base-url http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1`,
   `--model local-big`, concurrency 1 then 3.

**Gate:** report committed. Failures at concurrency 3 are expected; they are
the baseline.

## Phase 1 — LM Studio beside the current stack

Localhost only. Nothing else changes yet.

**Checked on this Mac (llmster 0.0.27-1, 2026-10-31):**
- Installer: `curl -fsSL https://lmstudio.ai/install.sh` → `~/.lmstudio/bin/lms`.
  Run with `--no-modify-path`; scripts call the full path.
- `lms load` flags: `--identifier`, `-c/--context-length`, `--gpu max`,
  `--parallel <n>` (concurrent predictions is a **load** flag), `--ttl`,
  `--estimate-only`, `-y`.
- `lms server start` flags: `-p/--port`, `--bind` (default `127.0.0.1`;
  `0.0.0.0` for LAN), `--cors`. **No auth flag**; auth and tokens are
  GUI-only (see D6).
- Runtime: `lms runtime get mlx-llm -y`.
- Weights: `lms get <hugging face URL> -y` picks an exact build; a bare
  staff-pick name with `-y` may pick a larger quantisation.

1. Install LM Studio 0.4.x (or `llmster`, per D6). Record the version in
   `docs/MODELS.md`.
2. Download the MLX 4-bit build of the current `local-big` weights and the
   embedding model. Exact model keys go in `config/roles.tsv` and
   `docs/MODELS.md` only.
3. Create `config/roles.tsv` (tab-separated, `#` comments allowed):
   ```
   # role        backend    model_key                 ctx    slots
   local-big     lmstudio   <mlx model key>           131072 3
   local-embed   lmstudio   <embedding model key>     2048   1
   cloud-cheap   gemini     <gemini model id>         -      -
   ```
4. Write `scripts/lms-load.sh`: reads `config/roles.tsv`, unloads anything not
   listed, loads each `lmstudio` row with `--identifier <role>` and
   `--context-length <ctx>` and GPU offload max. Set concurrent predictions to
   `slots` (confirm whether that is a load flag or a server setting).
   Idempotent.
5. Server settings: port 1234, **serve on local network OFF** for now, require
   authentication **ON**, CORS **OFF**, JIT model loading **OFF**, auto-unload
   **OFF**, "allow calling servers from mcp.json" **OFF**.
6. Create a host token; store it as `LMSTUDIO_HOST_TOKEN` in
   `~/.config/hearth/env` (mode 600).
7. Run `scripts/tool-smoke.sh` and `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh` against
   `http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1` at concurrency 1 and 3.
8. Stop Ollama's runner while benching (`ollama stop` or unload) so both
   servers are not competing for GPU memory; note it in the report.

**Gate (all must hold):**
- tool-smoke passes.
- Concurrency 3 × 10 rounds: zero timeouts, zero invalid tool calls.
- p95 TTFT at concurrency 3 is better than the phase 0 baseline.
- Peak footprint stays at least 4 GB under the current wired limit. If not,
  drop to 2 slots (D2) and rerun.
- A request with no token gets 401.

## Phase 2 — GPU headroom (optional, measure)

1. Read the current limit: `sysctl iogpu.wired_limit_mb` (0 means the default,
   ~75% of RAM).
2. Try `sudo sysctl iogpu.wired_limit_mb=56000` and rerun the phase 1
   concurrency bench.
3. If it helps, add a root launchd daemon `com.hearth.wired-limit` that sets
   it at boot (`scripts/install-wired-limit.sh --apply/--check/--remove`). It
   resets on reboot otherwise.

**Gate:** bench numbers recorded with and without. Keep only if better and
the system stays responsive with Zed, a browser and the bench running.

## Phase 3 — Host clients

1. **Zed (`settings.json`)** — replace the `hearth` provider:
   ```json
   "language_models": {
     "openai_compatible": {
       "hearth": {
         "api_url": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1",
         "available_models": [
           { "name": "local-big", "display_name": "local-big", "max_tokens": 131072 }
         ]
       }
     }
   }
   ```
   Token goes in through the Zed UI (keychain). Set the agent default, thread
   summary model, commit message model and inline assistant model to
   `hearth/local-big` so nothing asks for another model. Remove `cloud-cheap`
   from the host per D3. Update `docs/IDES.md`.
2. **MCP** — move `auslaw` from `litellm/config.yaml` into Zed
   `context_servers` as a stdio server with the same allowed tools and without
   the jade cookie. Update `docs/MCP.md` (registry stays; "keys" column becomes
   "clients").
3. **aider / aliases** — rewrite `scripts/aliases.zsh`:
   - `HEARTH_LOCAL=http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1`, token from
     `LMSTUDIO_HOST_TOKEN`, injected per invocation (keep the no-export rule).
   - `ask`, `ask-local`, `ask-big`, `ask-chat`, `ask-fast` all →
     `openai/local-big` (D1). `ask-arch` becomes `--architect` with both models
     `local-big` (same model, no eviction).
   - `ask-cloud` reads the Gemini model id from `config/roles.tsv` and uses
     `GOOGLE_API_KEY` directly. It is the only alias that can leave the host.
   - `ai-loaded` → `lms ps`. `ai-restart` → restart LM Studio's server.
4. **Open WebUI** (`scripts/webui.sh`) — point at
   `http://host.containers.internal:1234/v1` with the host token. Confirm
   containers can reach the host port with "serve on local network" still off;
   if not, note it and revisit in phase 4.
5. **Sandbox** — `scripts/sandboxed.sh` and `scripts/sandbox-probe.sh`: egress
   allow-list changes from `127.0.0.1:4000` to `127.0.0.1:1234`. The probe must
   still prove that nothing else is reachable.

**Gate:** a real Zed agent session (≥ 20 tool calls, two threads at once)
completes with no stall; aider `ask` and `ask-cloud` work; sandbox-probe
passes; WebUI answers.

## Phase 4 — LAN

**Decided 2026-10-31 (D8): SSH tunnel only.** The steps below describe LAN
serving and are kept for the record; what was built instead:

- Host stays bound to `127.0.0.1` (Serve on Local Network off).
- `scripts/client-setup.sh <name>` prints laptop steps; `--authorize` adds the
  laptop's key to `authorized_keys` as
  `command="/usr/bin/false",restrict,port-forwarding,permitopen="127.0.0.1:1234",permitopen="127.0.0.1:3000",permitlisten="none:1"`.
  `permitlisten` was added the same day after review: `permitopen` limits `-L` only, and `port-forwarding` had re-enabled `-R`.
  OpenSSH 10.5 rejects a port of 0 in that option (`invalid permission port`), so the dummy listen is `none:1`, a host the client never sends.
  The earlier throwaway-sshd test still holds for the forced command (shell refused, `-L` to :8888 refused, :1234 and :3000 work). `permitlisten="none:1"` was retested the same way: `-R` is refused and `-L` to :1234 still works.
- `client/hearth-client.sh` / `.ps1`: `ai-tunnel`, `ai-ping`, `ai-models`,
  `ai-ask`, `ai-webui`, `ask*` → `local-big`, `ask-cloud` with the laptop's
  own key. Secrets read from mode-600 files per command, never exported.
  `client/hearth-webui.sh` removed — the laptop uses the host's WebUI.
- `docs/LAPTOP.md` rewritten.

**Gate (adjusted for the tunnel):** from the laptop — `ai-ping` up, a wrong
token refused, `ai-models` lists the roles, a Zed agent session works,
`ask-cloud` works with the laptop's key, the tunnel key cannot open a shell.
From the host — `healthcheck.sh` section 5 still reports no LAN answer.


1. LM Studio: serve on local network **ON**. Authentication stays on.
2. One token per machine: `laptop-<name>`. Store on the laptop in
   `~/.hearth-key` (mode 600).
3. macOS firewall: allow LM Studio incoming. Never port-forward 1234.
4. Rewrite `client/hearth-client.sh`, `client/hearth-client.ps1`,
   `client/hearth-webui.sh`, `scripts/client-setup.sh`:
   - `HEARTH_GATEWAY` → `HEARTH_HOST` (e.g. `http://192.168.1.10:1234`); keep
     reading the old variable name for one release.
   - `ai-ping` checks `/v1/models` with the token (200 = up, 401 = bad token,
     no answer = host asleep).
   - `ai-models` lists identifiers from `/v1/models`.
   - `ask-cloud` on the laptop uses the laptop's own `GOOGLE_API_KEY`.
5. Off-LAN: document `ssh -N -L 1234:127.0.0.1:1234 USER@HOST`.
6. Rewrite `docs/LAPTOP.md` (Zed block, aider block, tunnel).

**Gate:** from the laptop — `ai-ping` up, request without token refused,
Zed agent session works, `ask-cloud` works with the laptop's key. From the
host — `nc -z <lan-ip> 1234` answers and an unauthenticated request is 401.

## Phase 5 — Retire the old stack, rewrite tooling

**Done early (2026-10-31, operator):** Ollama stopped (`launchctl bootout`) and
disabled (`launchctl disable gui/<uid>/com.colinnevin.ollama`); `Ollama.app` in
the Trash. Plist and model weights untouched. Rollback: Put Back the app,
`launchctl enable …`, then `launchctl bootstrap gui/<uid>
~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.colinnevin.ollama.plist`.

1. Stop and disable (do not uninstall yet): LiteLLM launchd agent, Ollama
   launchd service, `com.hearth.mlx-watchdog`, the Postgres container. Back up
   the Postgres volume to `~/hearth-backups/` first.
2. Delete scripts that only exist for the gateway or Ollama:
   `create-keys.sh`, `db.sh`, `setup-prisma.sh`, `deploy.sh`,
   `audit-fallbacks.sh`, `audit-mcp-scope.sh`, `bisect-config.sh`,
   `install-ollama-service.sh`, `lock-ollama.sh`, `mlx-watchdog.sh`,
   `apply-nothink.sh`, `test-nothink.sh`, `usage.sh`.
3. Rewrite against LM Studio and `config/roles.tsv`:
   `lib.sh`, `boot.sh`, `install-autostart.sh`, `status.sh`, `diagnose.sh`,
   `healthcheck.sh`, `preflight.sh`, `bench.sh`, `tool-smoke.sh`,
   `pin-digests.sh` (per D4), `swap-role.sh`, `pull-candidates.sh`
   (`lms get`), `mlx-bench.sh`.
   - `healthcheck.sh` must check: server up; each role answers; request
     without token → 401; LAN interface answers only with token; nothing but
     LM Studio listens on 1234; JIT loading is off (a request for an unlisted
     model fails rather than loading it).
     *Measured 2026-10-31: it does not fail — LM Studio answers an unknown
     model name with the loaded LLM. Nothing loads; healthcheck warns.*
   - `preflight.sh` keeps CRLF / bash-3.2 scans; replaces config checks with
     `config/roles.tsv` ↔ `docs/MODELS.md` consistency and the D4 drift check.
4. Keep unchanged: `searxng.sh`, `install-global-rules.sh`.
5. Remove `litellm/` from the tree (history keeps it).
6. Docs: rewrite `README.md`, `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/ROUTING.md`,
   `docs/RUNBOOK.md`, `docs/MODELS.md`, `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` (privacy is now
   "local server holds only local models" + sandbox egress, not key scope).
   Mark ADRs 0002, 0003, 0008, 0011, 0013, 0015 superseded by 0016.
7. `CLAUDE.md` hard rules:
   - Rule 1: concrete names in `config/roles.tsv` and `docs/MODELS.md`;
     MCP servers in Zed config docs and `docs/MCP.md`.
   - Rule 2: no router means no fallbacks. Replace with "LM Studio never holds
     a cloud model, and no script adds a remote backend to `config/roles.tsv`
     except under `backend: gemini` (or another explicit cloud backend)".
   - Rule 3: restate as above.
   - Rule 6: per D4.
   - Rule 7: update the resident set figure from the phase 1 measurement.

**Gate:** `preflight.sh` and `healthcheck.sh` pass on a clean reboot with
only LM Studio running. `grep -rn '4000\|11434\|litellm\|ollama'` over
`scripts/ client/ docs/*.md` returns only intentional historical mentions.

## Phase 6 — Soak and clean up

After the soak agreed in D7 with no stall:

1. Uninstall LiteLLM (pipx), Ollama, the Podman Postgres container and volume
   (backup kept), and delete Ollama model weights.
2. Remove the stopped launchd plists.
3. Mark ADR 0016 accepted; update `docs/PLAN.md`.

If a stall recurs during the soak: capture `lms log stream` output, footprint,
and the concurrency bench at the failing settings before changing anything.
# MLX stage-1 bench (Ollama vs Rapid-MLX)

> **Historical.** Rapid-MLX vs Ollama (ADR 0012/0013). Local inference is oMLX — ADR 0026.

Compare **agent tool loops** — not raw tok/s. Zed needs read → tool result →
write with messy file content. Code bench (`scripts/bench.sh`) does not catch
the Qwen XML failure mode.

**Date drafted:** 2026-09-06. **2026-10-22:** `local-big` moved back to Ollama's
MLX runner after the same tool-smoke passed there (ADR 0013). This file is for
a side experiment. It is not how the role is served.

## Envelope (this Mac)

| Constraint | Value |
|---|---|
| Unified RAM | 64 GB |
| GPU-accessible | ~48 GB |
| Hot models | 1 Ollama model (ADR 0011). A Rapid-MLX process beside it spends the GPU budget |

| MLX alias (Rapid-MLX) | Weights (approx) | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `qwen3.6-35b-4bit` | ~20 GB | bench only — role is the Ollama tag in `docs/MODELS.md` |
| `gpt-oss-20b` | ~13 GB | bench / break-glass |
| `gemma-4-12b-4bit` | ~8 GB | candidate — not promoted |

## Install Rapid-MLX

```bash
scripts/mlx-bench.sh --install-rapid
scripts/mlx-bench.sh --serve qwen3.6-35b-4bit   # foreground, :8000, not launchd
```

Stop it when the experiment is done so it does not sit beside Ollama.

**Gemma 4** MLX weights need the vision stack:

```bash
pipx inject rapid-mlx 'mlx-vlm==0.6.17'
```

## Tool smoke (automated)

Latest results: `bench/results/2026-09-06-1229-tool-smoke/`

```bash
scripts/tool-smoke.sh openai http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1 qwen3.6-35b-4bit
scripts/tool-smoke.sh ollama gpt-oss:20b
scripts/tool-smoke.sh ollama qwen3-coder:30b
```

Pass = **turn 2 emits `write_file` or `edit_file`** with `<` in content.

## Zed

Use the **`hearth`** provider — agent default `local-big`, which is Ollama
(ADR 0013).

For raw MLX A/B (bypass gateway):

```json
"mlx": {
  "api_url": "http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1",
  "available_models": [
    { "name": "qwen3.6-35b-4bit", "display_name": "qwen3.6-35b-4bit (MLX direct)", "max_tokens": 65536 }
  ]
}
```

## Decision rules

| Outcome | Action |
|---|---|
| Candidate fails tool smoke | Do not promote |
| Passes + Zed manual OK | ADR + `docs/MODELS.md` + config. Qwen3.6 is already `local-big` via Ollama (ADR 0013) |

Nothing else becomes a role until `scripts/swap-role.sh` + ADR.
# Model registry

Role → model. This file and `config/roles.tsv` are the only places a concrete
model name is written by hand. Update both together, plus an ADR. Generated
copies: Zed `display_name` labels (`scripts/zed-sync.sh`) and the key plus
weights path in `pins/digests.tsv` (`scripts/pin-digests.sh`). (Before ADR 0016
the second file was `litellm/config.yaml`.)

**Hardware envelope:** the ceiling is installed RAM minus 8 GB (ADR 0031):
**56 GB** on the 64 GB host, **40 GB** on a 48 GB host. `iogpu.wired_limit_mb`
and the oMLX guard both follow it. macOS's default GPU cap (~75% of RAM)
applies until `scripts/iogpu-wired.sh` runs. `local-deep` evicts
`local-main` for its session on every tier (ADR 0033). `local-edit` runs on
a laptop's own oMLX, not on the host. The resident set matters more than any
single model's size — see the measurement below.

## Live — oMLX (ADR 0026)

oMLX serves these from `~/.hearth/models`. Machine-read copy:
`config/roles.tsv`. The `weights` directory names are the source repos.

| Role | Model key | Source | Size | Load | Set | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `local-main` | `qwen3.6-35b-a3b-mlx` | `lmstudio-community/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MLX-4bit` @ `0c4a20a` | 20.4 GB | 128K × 3 parallel, no draft, **thinking on (set by `scripts/omlx.sh`)** | 2026-11-06 | MoE, ~3B active, 4-bit. Returned after the 2026-11-06 benches: faster decode, the better long-context answer, c3 30/30 with TTFT p50 60.7 s against the previous resident's 79.2 s. Short tasks emit about 2.2× the tokens. Peak 34.0 GB. ADR 0030. Native window 262,144 |
| `local-deep` | `qwen3.8-27b-mlx` | `lmstudio-community/Qwen3.8-27B-MLX-4bit` @ `6067b15` | 16.1 GB | 64K × 1, **on demand**: `ask-deep` loads it beside `local-main` and unloads it on the way out | 2026-11-01 | Dense 27B. Best `bench/tasks` 03 answer of the three benched; ~26 tok/s and 2.5–5.5× the tokens of the 26B-A4B, so 10–24× slower. For hard one-off questions, never agent loops. Thinks by default (its template). Beside the resident, both peaked at 49.9 GB on 2026-11-01, over the old ~48 GB cap, and the resident's requests timed out during a long prefill. The cap is now 56 GB, so the two may be loaded together on 64 GB (ADR 0028). On a 48 GB host (40 GB ceiling) `ask-deep` evicts `local-main` instead (ADR 0031). ADR 0020 |
| `local-edit` | `sweep-next-edit-1.5b-mlx-8bit` | `sweepai/sweep-next-edit-1.5B` @ `4090165` (`sweep-next-edit-1.5b.q8_0.v2.gguf`, sha256 `1321ea5e…`), tokenizer rebuilt from the GGUF's pruned 43,839-token vocabulary (template `Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B` @ `df3ce67`), converted to MLX 8-bit at `~/.hearth/models/hearth/sweep-next-edit-1.5B-MLX-8bit` | 1.4 GB | 8K × 2, **edit**: laptop oMLX only, `/v1/completions`, greedy, 512 max tokens | 2026-11-07 | Editor edit prediction (Zed `open_ai_compatible_api`, prompt format `sweep`). Apache 2.0, Qwen2.5-Coder base. Laptop bench 2026-11-07: p50 350 / p95 398 ms, 24/30 (misses `02-add-field`). Host alone p95 281 ms; beside c3 p95 5747 ms and `local-main` TTFT p95 89.7 s — **host does not load it** (ADR 0033 amend). Paid Zed Zeta is a separate cloud opt-in (`docs/IDES.md`) |
| `cloud-cheap` | `gemini-3.6-flash` | Google, `ask-cloud` only | — | — | 2026-10-22 | 1M ctx, 65K output. ADR 0014 |

**Resident set:** concurrency-3 peak 34.0 GB at 128K × 3, ~30K-token prompts
(`bench/results/2026-11-06-2012-concurrency-c3-qwen3.6`, oMLX process
footprint). The previous resident the same day peaked at 31.0 GB
(`bench/results/2026-11-06-1956-concurrency-c3-local-main`). Loaded beside
`local-deep` was not re-measured; the last combined peak was 49.9 GB with
the smaller resident (ADR 0028).

**Thinking is set by `scripts/omlx.sh`.** `local-main` gets `enable_thinking`
and the qwen reasoning parser. Sampling follows the weights' generation
config: temperature 1.0, top-k 20, top-p 0.95, repetition penalty 1.0. Zed
also pins `temperature: 1.0` for `local-main` in `agent.model_parameters`.
The previous resident looped in thinking at a lower temperature (Zed agent
turns, 2026-11-01); that sampling does not follow the role.

`scripts/omlx.sh up` writes those settings again. `scripts/healthcheck.sh` fails if
`local-main` returns no `reasoning_content` or leaks thought markers into the
answer. Without thinking it answered a simple change sum wrongly three times.

`local-code` and `local-chat` were collapsed into `local-big` (now `local-main`, ADR 0021) on 2026-10-31
(D1). The Ollama weights were deleted the same day with the rest of the old
stack.

**Weight drift:** recorded and checked — see Weight pinning below (D4).

**Bench candidate (not a role):** `qwen3.6:27b-coding-mtp-q4_K_M` on Ollama
(~18 GB) — rejected for verbosity vs `qwen3-coder:30b` (ADR 0005 era bench).

## Tiers (ADR 0032)

`config/roles.tsv` column 9, `min_ram`: per role, a host serves the row with
the largest `min_ram` at or below its RAM. `HEARTH_RAM_GB` overrides the
detected size. Ceiling is RAM minus 8 (ADR 0031).

| RAM | Ceiling | `local-main` | `local-deep` | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| < 48 GB | — | none: `omlx.sh up` refuses | — | no tier benched |
| 48 GB and up | RAM − 8 | `qwen3.6-35b-a3b-mlx`, 128K × 3 | `qwen3.8-27b-mlx`, **swap** | live on 64 GB. 48 GB waits on a c3 bench on 48 GB hardware with the host's `\.omlx/` matcher: 30/30, peak ≤ 36 GB. `local-edit` is laptop-only (ADR 0033 amend) |

### Candidates by RAM (unbenched)

Not roles. Each needs `bench/tasks` and `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh` on
that hardware before it gets a row (rule 5). Web search, 2026-11-07. Sources
are mostly blogs, Hugging Face cards and leaderboards, and they often
disagree: sizes are as reported unless marked *(repo)*, which means measured
here. Usable = RAM minus 8 (ADR 0031); weights plus KV cache must fit under
it. *Agent* = MoE with ~3–4B active, for resident tool loops. *Deep* =
slower and stronger, for one-off questions.

**Shortlist to bench first**

| RAM (usable) | Agent | Deep | Load |
|---|---|---|---|
| 32 GB (24) | Gemma 4 26B-A4B | Devstral Small 2 24B or Qwen3.8-27B | swap |
| 48 GB (40) | Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (tier row) | Qwen3.8-27B (tier row) | swap |
| 64 GB (56) | Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (tier row); alternative Qwen3-Coder-Next 80B-A3B with no deep beside | Qwen3.8-27B (tier row) | beside |
| 128 GB (120) | Qwen3.6 35B-A3B | Qwen3.5-122B-A10B or gpt-oss-120b | beside (~109 GB with the 122B, tight) |
| 256 GB (248) | Qwen3.6 35B-A3B | DeepSeek V4 Flash | beside |
| 512 GB (504) | Qwen3.6 35B-A3B | MiniMax M3 (beside) or GLM-5.3 (swap) | see below |

**Quant recipe A/B (same bases as live roles)**

Not new families — alternate MLX quants of the incumbent bases, recorded
2026-11-07 from a web scan (`mlx-alt-weights-research` canvas). Goal: see
whether a mixed-precision recipe beats the live `lmstudio-community` MLX-4bit
pins on `bench/tasks`, tool-smoke, and `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh` under
the RAM-minus-8 ceiling. Download under `~/.hearth/models/<weights>` (HF
org/name). Do **not** point `roles.tsv` at these until a bench wins (rule 5).

| Priority | Challenges | Weights path (HF) | Kind | Size (card) | Why |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `local-main` | `Jundot/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-oQ4e-mtp` | oQe mixed (oMLX 0.7.3), MTP + vision | ~22 GB | oMLX-author quant; r/oMLX prefers oQ for this server |
| 2 | `local-main` | `unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-MLX-4bit` | Unsloth UD dynamic 4-bit | ~23 GB class | Often ranked above native mlx-community 4-bit |
| 3 | `local-main` | `mlx-community/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-OptiQ-4bit` | OptiQ mixed 4/8, MTP sidecar | ~22 GB | Sensitivity-aware; Capability Score claims vs uniform-4bit |
| control | `local-main` | `mlx-community/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-4bit` | uniform mlx-vlm 4-bit | 20.4 GB | Same size class as the live pin — publisher/control twin |
| 1 | `local-deep` | `Jundot/Qwen3.6-27B-oQ4e-mtp` | oQ4e imatrix + MTP | ~17 GB | Dense 27B class; their table edged mlx-lm 4bit avg |
| later | either | `nightmedia/*-qx64-hi-mlx` (matching base) | Deckard hybrid qx | varies | Obscure hybrid; tool-smoke first |
| catalog | either | `majentik/garden` TurboQuant / RotorQuant tiers | smoke-gated | varies | Only after a named repo is picked; verify text vs full tower |

Live pins stay: `lmstudio-community/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-MLX-4bit` (`local-main`),
`lmstudio-community/Qwen3.8-27B-MLX-4bit` (`local-deep`). Skip GGUF orgs for
oMLX (no GGUF→MLX path).

**Alternatives by tier**

