`local-main` stays a ~3B-active MoE at every tier: wall time is set by active
parameters and tokens emitted (see *Verbosity, not speed*). More RAM buys a
better `local-deep`, keeps it loaded, and adds slots. Large `local-deep`
models prefill slowly; they suit short hard questions, not long context.

### Building `local-edit` (ADR 0033)

On the host, or on a laptop for its own oMLX. Downloads ~1.5 GB of GGUF
and the base model's tokenizer files into `~/.hearth/models/.src`.

```bash
client/edit-model.sh convert sweepai/sweep-next-edit-1.5B 409016591c6c1a94f545f22328a85a3516118f34 \
  sweep-next-edit-1.5b.q8_0.v2.gguf Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B df3ce67c0e24480f20468b6ef2894622d69eb73b
```

Then pin on the host (`scripts/pin-digests.sh`) so digests stay shared. Serve
and bench on a laptop: `client/edit-model.sh laptop-up`, then
`client/edit-model.sh bench`. Host `omlx.sh up` does not load `local-edit`.

### Edit prediction candidates (unbenched)

For Zed's `open_ai_compatible_api` edit prediction provider (`docs/IDES.md`).
No role yet: a `local-edit` row needs a latency bench, an ADR, and a check
that predictions do not starve agent turns of oMLX's request slots. Web
search, 2026-11-07. Scores are each vendor's own benchmark and are not
comparable across vendors.

| Model | Size | Zed prompt format | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Sweep Next-Edit 1.5B** (`sweepai/sweep-next-edit-1.5B`) — now `local-edit` | ~1.6 GB at Q8; under 1 GB at 4-bit | `sweep` | First pick. Apache 2.0, Qwen2.5-Coder base, 8K context. Sweep's benchmark: 67.8% against 43.3% for Zed's hosted model. Under 500 ms on a laptop with speculative decoding (vendor). No MLX build found; would be converted with `mlx_lm.convert` and pinned |
| Sweep Next-Edit v2 7B | ~4.5 GB at 4-bit | `sweep` | Higher quality (v1 7B scored 81.3% on the same benchmark). Community MLX fp16 conversion exists. Fits 48 GB beside `local-main` only just |
| Zeta 2.1 (`zed-industries/zeta-2.1`) | 8B (Seed-Coder base), ~5 GB at 4-bit | `zeta2_1` | Zed's own model, Zed's native prompt format. Community MLX 4-bit conversion |
| Instinct (`continuedev/instinct`) | 7B (Qwen2.5-Coder) | none built in | Apache 2.0. Sweep reports it repeats itself ~10% of the time. Not a fit for Zed's formats |
| Qwen2.5-Coder 1.5B base | ~1 GB at 4-bit | `qwen` | Plain fill-in-the-middle, not next-edit. Baseline only |

## Dormant

Four roles, no keys: `cloud-code`, `cloud-frontier`, `cloud-frontier-direct`, `cloud-frontier-fast`. In the gateway era these were commented-out blocks in `config.yaml`;
after ADR 0016 a cloud role would be a `gemini` (or other explicit cloud
backend) row in `config/roles.tsv` plus an alias that passes its own key —
never an `omlx` row. A remote model on `:1234` would break the boundary.

| Role | Model | Blocked on |
|---|---|---|
| `cloud-code` | `claude-sonnet-5` | Anthropic API key — **deliberately unset**. A Console key bills separately from any Claude subscription. Only worth opening if aider, Zed or a laptop should reach Claude through the gateway |
| `cloud-frontier` | `kimi-k3` via OpenRouter | OpenRouter key. Primary frontier route |
| `cloud-frontier-direct` | `kimi-k3` via Moonshot | Moonshot key. Same price, best K3 caching |
| `cloud-frontier-fast` | `kimi-k3` via Fireworks | Fireworks key. Dearest; throughput or LoRA only |


There is **no local Kimi K3**. The smallest community quant is ~540 GB at 1.56
bits, and Ollama cannot load it — support is an open llama.cpp pull request.
Frontier is cloud-only by necessity, which is what these roles are for.

## Retired

| Model | Was | Removed | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| `gemma-4-26b-a4b-it-qat-mlx` (`lmstudio-community/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-QAT-MLX-4bit` @ `aa5e2e5`) | `local-main` | 2026-11-06 | Resident from ADR 0024. Short tasks finished sooner and the weights are smaller. Lost the 2026-11-06 long-context answer and the c3 prefill (TTFT p50 79.2 s, peak 31.0 GB). Weights kept. ADR 0030 |
| `qwen3.6:35b-mlx` (Ollama) | `local-big` | 2026-10-31 | Ollama build of the same 35B-A3B, now `local-main` again. ADR 0016, 0030 |
| `qwen3-coder:30b` | `local-code` | 2026-10-31 | Role collapsed into `local-big` (D1). ADR 0016 |
| `qwen3:8b` | `local-chat` | 2026-10-31 | Role collapsed into `local-big` (D1). ADR 0016 |
| `nomic-embed-text` (Ollama, then an f16 GGUF) | `local-embed` | 2026-11-01 | Nothing called it. Healthcheck was the only request. ADR 0018 |
| `gpt-oss:20b` | `local-big` | 2026-09-06 | Slower tool-smoke than the Qwen3.6 agent. ADR 0012, then 0013 |
| `llama3.3:70b` | `local-big` | 2026-08-02 | 42 GB, could not stay resident. 440 s to first reply. ADR 0004 |
| `qwen2.5-coder:32b` | `local-code` | 2026-08-02 | Superseded by the 30B MoE at the same size |
| `qwen2.5-coder:7b` | `local-fast` | 2026-08-02 | Role retired — the MoE made a separate fast coder redundant. ADR 0005 |

Note on the last one: Qwen3-Coder has **no variant below 30B**, so
`qwen2.5-coder:7b` remained the best sub-10 GB coding model available. It went
because the role became unnecessary, not because it was outdated. If a small
dense coder appears, the role can return.

## Verbosity, not speed

Second measurement, from the first real bench run:

| Model | tokens over 4 tasks | wall clock | avg tok/s |
|---|---|---|---|
| `qwen3-coder:30b` | 1,218 | 11.2 s | 108.8 |
| `gpt-oss:20b` | 4,466 (3.7x) | 47.6 s | 93.8 |
| `qwen3:8b` (thinking on) | 13,840 (11.4x) | 181.4 s | 76.3 |

Throughput barely moves — 65–120 tok/s across everything, because the machine
is memory-bandwidth bound. **Wall clock is set by how many tokens a model emits,
not how fast.** A hybrid reasoning model can be the slowest role while being the
smallest.

Disabling thinking on `local-chat` took it from 2,370 tokens / 29.7 s to
**386 tokens / 5.1 s** through the gateway. See ADR 0006.

## The measurement that drove all of this

Through the gateway, one short prompt each, from cold:

| Role | Before | Time | After | Time |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `local-code` | qwen2.5-coder:32b | 29 s | qwen3-coder:30b | **6 s** |
| `local-big` | llama3.3:70b | **440 s** | gpt-oss:20b | **5 s** |
| `cloud-cheap` | gemini-3.5-flash | 2 s | gemini-3.5-flash | 1 s |

**440 s to 5 s — not a faster model, a resident one.**

The old set needed 65.7 GB against a ~48 GB GPU ceiling, so something was always
evicted and reaching for `local-big` meant reloading 42 GB from disk. That
Ollama-era set totalled 37.5 GB. The live resident set is the figure at the
top of this file.

The lesson worth keeping: on a fixed-memory machine the **resident set** matters
more than any individual model's quality. A 70B that evicts your coding model is
slower in practice than a 20B that never leaves RAM, whatever the benchmarks say.
MoE architectures are what make the newer set fit — ~3B active parameters gives
near-30B reasoning at 7B speed.

Disk: 63 GB before, ~37 GB after.

## Cost reference

Verified 2026-08-01. LiteLLM auto-syncs its own cost map; this table is for human
decisions and does **not** update itself. Re-check before relying on it.

| Kimi K3 via | $/M in | $/M out | Note |
|---|---|---|---|
| Moonshot (direct) | 3.00 | 15.00 | source of truth |
| OpenRouter | 3.00 | 15.00 | ~92% cache hit reported → effective input ≈ 0.52 |
| Fireworks serverless | 3.30 | 16.50 | 10% dearer; buys throughput and LoRA |

- **Fireworks is the dearest of the three.** This stack originally defaulted to it
  because it was the provider that came to mind first, not because it won on
  price. That is exactly the failure mode this registry exists to catch.
- **Output price is the one that bites** — it compounds through agentic loops.

## Weight pinning (D4)

`scripts/pin-digests.sh` hashes each role's `weights` path (column 6 of
`config/roles.tsv`, under `~/.hearth/models`) into `pins/digests.tsv`:
file count, bytes, newest mtime, and a sha256 over every file. Git-tracked,
so drift shows in a diff.

```bash
scripts/pin-digests.sh                  # record, after a bench you trust (~40 s)
scripts/pin-digests.sh --check          # fast; preflight runs this
scripts/pin-digests.sh --check --deep   # re-hash everything
```

Recorded 2026-10-31. This detects, it does not enforce — oMLX loads
whatever is on disk. The embedding pin was removed with the role (ADR 0018).

## Changing a role

1. Put the candidate weights under `~/.hearth/models` at the source path in
   this file
2. Bench it against the incumbent: `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh` and
   `bench/tasks/` (rule 5). Same settings as the last baseline
3. Check the footprint fits under the ceiling (RAM minus 8: 56 GB on 64, 40 GB on 48) with `local-main` already resident
4. Update this table and `config/roles.tsv` together (row at the tier's `min_ram`, ADR 0032), then `scripts/omlx.sh up`
   and `scripts/zed-sync.sh`
5. `scripts/tool-smoke.sh openai http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1 local-main`
6. Write an ADR in `docs/decisions/` and a row in `docs/HISTORY.md`
7. Delete the old weights only once the replacement has proven itself

`scripts/bench.sh` runs `bench/tasks` against a resident role. It does not
load a candidate by itself.
# Build status

> **Historical (gateway era).** Current design: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`. What was tried: `docs/HISTORY.md`.

Last updated: 2026-08-02

**Not the source of truth.** Current design: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` and
`docs/MODELS.md`. This file is the gateway-era plan. The original plan is
preserved in `docs/decisions/0001-inherited-state.md`.

## Where things stand

| Phase | State |
|---|---|
| 0 — capture inherited state | done — ADR 0001 |
| 1 — repo scaffold | done |
| 2 — role-based gateway config | **deployed and serving** |
| Postgres via Podman on :5433 | done, running |
| Virtual keys | done — four created, scoped |
| Leak test | **passing** — boundary enforced, not just documented |
| Aliases switched to roles | done |
| 3 — lock Ollama to localhost | **done** — LAN unreachable, verified |
| llama3.3:70b retired | done — ADR 0004. 440 s to 5 s |
| 4 — bench harness | done and run — found the local-chat verbosity problem |
| local-chat thinking disabled | done — ADR 0006. 29.7 s to 5.1 s |
| digest pinning | recorded + drift-checked (not enforceable in Ollama) |
| 5 — auto-update pipeline | deferred |
| 6 — Open WebUI | **done** — chat UI on :3000, private-scoped, local-chat default |
| Web search via SearXNG | **done** — opt-in per message, :8888, verified end to end |
| `usage.sh` visibility | done — role, tier, model, tokens, LOCAL/CLOUD split |
| 7 — verification tooling | done — preflight, deploy, healthcheck, audit |

## What changed from the original plan

- **Frontier providers: three, not one.** Fireworks was the original default purely
  because it was the provider first mentioned. Verified 2026-08-01, it is the
  dearest: $3.30/$16.50 versus $3.00/$15.00 from both OpenRouter and Moonshot
  direct. OpenRouter is now primary — one key, day-zero access to new models.
- **`cloud-code` is dormant by choice.** No Anthropic API key. A Console key bills
  separately from a Claude subscription, so the gateway route only earns its
  place if aider, Zed or a laptop should reach Claude through the gateway.
- **Four keys, not three.** Added `lan-cloud` so the laptop can reach cloud models
  without widening the everyday `lan` key.
- **Postgres in Podman, not Docker Desktop.**
- **`ask` now defaults to local.** The old `ask` pointed at Anthropic with no key
  set, so it was broken. The safe default on a privacy-first stack should be the
  private one; cloud is now the explicit reach (`ask-cloud`).
- **Deployment does not touch launchd.** `run-litellm.sh` already loads
  `~/.config/litellm/config.yaml`, so that path becomes a symlink into the repo.
  Rollback is one `mv`.

## Bugs the harness caught before deploy

Kept as a record of why the tooling exists.

1. **Fallback leak** in the inherited config — `local-ollama → gemini` meant any
   Ollama timeout silently sent the prompt to Google.
2. **`ollama/` vs `ollama_chat/`** — the completion-API prefix causes tool-call
   loops and dropped context.
3. **Preflight reported a fake leak** when PyYAML was missing. A check that cannot
   run must never look like a finding; ERROR and FAIL are now distinct exit codes.
4. **Env check read the wrong environment** — it consulted the interactive shell,
   but launchd only sources `~/.config/litellm/env`. A false green.
5. **Model presence check was tag-naive** — `nomic-embed-text` did not match
   `nomic-embed-text:latest`.
6. **launchd detection** used `launchctl list | grep`, which misses gui-domain
   agents; it reported "not loaded" while the gateway was serving traffic.
7. **`db.sh` went missing** from disk and nothing noticed. Preflight now verifies
   every referenced script exists.
8. **Leak test could false-pass** — it probed a dormant role, where "model not
   found" also returns 4xx. Now probes a live cloud role and adds a positive
   control, since a merely broken key also refuses everything.
9. **`model_info: tier: local`** — `tier` is a real LiteLLM field accepting only
   `free` or `paid`. Pydantic rejected it and LiteLLM logged "ignoring and
   continuing with other deployments", silently dropping EVERY model. The
   gateway started clean, passed readiness, and served nothing.
10. **bash 4 syntax on bash 3.2** — `declare -A` and unguarded `"${arr[@]}"`
    pass everywhere except macOS, which is the only place it runs.
11. **`curl | python - args <<'PY'`** — the heredoc and the pipe both claim
    stdin, so `json.load(sys.stdin)` reads nothing. Symptom is a bogus
    "response was not JSON".
12. **`/key/delete` accepted a token hash and did nothing** — it wants
    `key_aliases`. A silent no-op that looks like success.
13. **Open WebUI env vars were overridden by its database.** Env only seeds a
    fresh DB; the persisted volume kept the first run's settings. `podman exec
    ... env` showed the feature enabled while the UI had it off.

## Next

1. **Re-run `bench.sh`** now that thinking is off, so the recorded numbers
   match reality, then **read the task 03 output.** `gpt-oss:20b` earned `local-big` on
   capacity, not quality — nothing has yet confirmed it reasons well. Task 03
   hides a float-for-currency bug in money arithmetic; a model that fixes
   the obvious `range(len())` and misses that is the wrong tool for
   production financial code. Syntax checks cannot answer this. It needs a human read.
2. **Record digests after that read**, with `scripts/pin-digests.sh`, so drift
   is detectable from a known-good state rather than an arbitrary one.
3. **Live with it for a week** before building the auto-updater. It is the
   piece most likely to be overbuilt for a problem that turns out to be small.

**Gaps identified 2026-08-02** by comparing against other setups
(`docs/COMPARISON.md`):

- ~~**No sandboxing.**~~ **Closed 2026-08-02** — `scripts/sandboxed.sh` denies
  egress to everything but the gateway and confines writes to the working
  directory. Verified on macOS 26.4, 10/10 checks. ADR 0007.
- ~~**No prompt-injection threat model.**~~ **Written** — `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`.
  Conclusion: detection does not work, so the model exists to say which
  capabilities to cut rather than what to filter.
- **Untrusted MCP servers are unaudited.** Any MCP that fetches from the open
  web and sits next to real client data needs a tool-surface audit before an
  agent is allowed to call it. Highest value per effort of anything left.
- **No confirmation gates** on consequential actions beyond git commits. Costs
  nothing today because nothing in the stack can send mail or move money.
- **No local embeddings.** `local-embed` was removed 2026-11-01 (ADR 0018).
  A reference corpus would be a new role, benched first.

Optional, when the need arises:

- Enable `cloud-code` if aider, Zed or a laptop should reach Claude — needs a
  Console API key, which bills separately from any subscription.
- Enable `cloud-frontier` (OpenRouter) for Kimi K3 and day-zero access to new
  models. There is no local K3 at any quantisation.
- A second Open WebUI without search configured, if you want "no web search on
  sensitive work" to be structural rather than remembered.
# Review handoff: the 2026-10-31 server cut

> **Historical.** Do not follow this as current setup. Serving is oMLX: `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` and `docs/RUNBOOK.md`.

For an AI reviewer (Cursor, Zed, Claude Code) working in a clone of this repo.
Written 2026-10-31. **Review only: change nothing, run nothing that mutates
state.** Report findings; the operator decides what gets fixed.

## Scope

```bash
git log --oneline 8e9ecf2..HEAD     # ~20 commits, all 2026-10-31
git diff --stat 8e9ecf2 HEAD        # bench/results/ is most of the line count — skim it
```

The hearth stack moved from **LiteLLM + Ollama + Postgres** (a gateway on
`:4000`) to **the LM Studio app serving directly on `127.0.0.1:1234`**. The
old stack is deleted, the scripts are rewritten, host clients are cut over,
and laptops reach the host over an SSH tunnel only. Read these first, in this
order:

1. `CLAUDE.md` — the hard rules. Everything is reviewed against them.
2. `docs/decisions/0016-lmstudio-direct.md` — the decision.
3. `docs/MIGRATION-LMSTUDIO.md` — phases, status table, decisions D1–D8.
4. `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/ROUTING.md`, `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`.

## What to review, highest risk first

| # | Area | Files | Questions |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Privacy by placement** | `config/roles.tsv`, `scripts/omlx.sh`, `scripts/aliases.zsh`, `client/*`, `scripts/webui.sh`, `scripts/sandboxed.sh` | Can anything sent to `:1234` reach a cloud model? Does any path other than `ask-cloud` carry a cloud key? Can a sandboxed agent reach anything but `127.0.0.1:1234`? |
| 2 | **Secrets** | `scripts/lib.sh` (`ai_load_env`, `hearth_http`), `client/hearth-client.sh` / `.ps1`, `scripts/aliases.zsh`, `scripts/preflight.sh` | Is a token or key ever exported, put on a command line (visible in `ps`), logged, or written somewhere other than a mode-600 file? `hearth_http` passes it via `curl -K -` for this reason. The `.ps1` sets `$env:` vars for the child — is the cleanup sound? |
| 3 | **Laptop access (D8)** | `scripts/client-setup.sh`, `docs/LAPTOP.md`, the `CLAUDE.md` "Which machine" block | The `authorized_keys` line is `command="/usr/bin/false",restrict,port-forwarding,permitopen=...`. Is the key really tunnel-only? Does `--authorize` refuse and back up correctly? Are the AI-assisted setup instructions safe for a cloud assistant (never sees a secret)? |
| 4 | **Server exposure** | `scripts/boot.sh`, `scripts/healthcheck.sh` | LM Studio rewrites its saved settings on `lms server start`. Is `127.0.0.1` + no CORS enforced on every start path, and checked? Does each negative test (401, no LAN, no CORS, JIT off) have a positive control? |
| 5 | **Login autostart** | `scripts/install-autostart.sh`, `scripts/boot.sh` | launchd `RunAtLoad` + `AbandonProcessGroup` (without it, launchd killed the Podman VM). Failure modes if LM Studio is slow to start, or Podman is missing? |
| 6 | **Exit codes** | every script | `0` pass, `1` real problem, `2` could not run. Can a missing dependency ever report as `1`, or a failure as `0`? |
| 7 | **Portability** | `scripts/*.sh`, `client/hearth-client.sh` | macOS `/bin/bash` 3.2: no `declare -A`, `mapfile`, `${x^^}`; empty arrays guarded under `set -u`. `client/hearth-client.sh` must run in bash *and* zsh on macOS and Linux. Python in heredocs: no heredoc + pipe both on stdin. |
| 8 | **Model-name rule** | everything | Concrete model names only in `config/roles.tsv` and `docs/MODELS.md` (plus generated Zed labels and redacted bench results). `grep` for any that leaked. |
| 9 | **Weight pinning (D4)** | `scripts/pin-digests.sh`, `pins/digests.tsv` | Does `--check` really detect a changed file? Does any doc imply roles are *immutable* rather than drift-detected? |
| 10 | **Docs drift** | `README.md`, `docs/*.md` | Any instruction still pointing at `:4000`, `:11434`, LiteLLM, virtual keys, Postgres, Ollama or `llmster` *as current*? Superseded ADRs and docs marked **Historical** are expected to mention them. Do the docs match what the scripts actually do? |
| 11 | **Benches** | `bench/concurrency.py`, `scripts/concurrency-bench.sh`, `bench/results/2026-10-31-*` | Do the reports' conclusions follow from the numbers? Is the c3 TTFT comparison (LM Studio vs Ollama) like for like? |

## Known and accepted — don't report these as new

- No soak week before deleting the old stack (operator's call). Rollback is
  not possible; ADR 0016 says so.
- With JIT off, an unknown model name is answered by `local-big` instead of
  refused. `healthcheck.sh` warns about it.
- LM Studio auth and tokens are GUI-only; there is no CLI (lmstudio-ai/lms#489).
- Prefill is serialised even with `--parallel 3`; recorded in the phase 0/1 reports.
- Phase 4's laptop gate has not been run yet.
- Cursor cannot use hearth as a model provider (its backend makes the calls).

## Also changed, outside the repo (host only)

Described so you don't have to look. Ask the operator before opening any of
these, and never open the ones in the next section.

- `~/.config/zed/settings.json` — `hearth` provider at `127.0.0.1:1234`,
  every model slot `hearth/local-main`. The legal-research profile recorded
  during phase 3 is no longer installed; `docs/MCP.md` preserves it as a
  worked setup example.
- `~/.aider.conf.yml` — `openai/local-big` for model and weak model.
- `~/.zshrc` — sources `scripts/aliases.zsh`; Ollama exports removed.
- `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.hearth.boot.plist` — written by `install-autostart.sh`.
- `~/.ssh/authorized_keys` — gets one restricted line per laptop.

## Off limits

You are probably a cloud model; whatever you read goes to your vendor.
**Never open, print, grep or copy:**

- `~/.config/hearth/env` — LM Studio token and Google key
- `~/.zshenv.local*` — a backup there still holds a key
- `~/hearth-backups/` — old database dumps
- `~/.lmstudio/.internal/` — server settings, token hashes
- `~/.hearth-key`, `~/.hearth-gemini-key`, `~/.ssh/` on any machine

## What you may run

Read-only, on the host only, all safe to repeat (exit codes as above):

```bash
scripts/preflight.sh
scripts/healthcheck.sh
scripts/install-autostart.sh --check
scripts/omlx.sh status
scripts/pin-digests.sh --check
scripts/zed-sync.sh --check
bash -n scripts/*.sh client/hearth-client.sh
```

**Don't run:** `boot.sh`, `omlx.sh up`,
`install-autostart.sh --apply/--remove`, `client-setup.sh --authorize/--install`,
`bench.sh`, `concurrency-bench.sh`,
`pin-digests.sh` (without `--check`), `zed-sync.sh` (without `--check`),
`podman`, `launchctl` or `sudo`.
On a laptop, run none of `scripts/`.

If you're Zed on `local-big` (128K context, ADR 0017), review one area from the table
per thread rather than the whole diff at once.

## How to report

One finding per item, most severe first:

- **Severity:** critical (privacy or secret leak) / high (wrong result,
  broken check) / medium / low (docs, style)
- **Where:** `path:line`
- **What:** one sentence
- **Failure scenario:** concrete inputs or state → what goes wrong
- **Confidence:** confirmed (you ran or traced it) / plausible

Say which areas you reviewed and which you didn't. "No findings" for an area
is a useful answer, but only with a line on what you checked.
# Routing rules

The decision table. When in doubt, local.

## What must stay local

Route to `local-*` roles (oMLX, `:1234`), always:

- Any repo or path you have flagged as holding sensitive or private data
- Names, addresses, contact details, financial figures, ID documents
- Anything covered by applicable privacy obligations
- Credentials, keys, `.env` contents, database dumps
- Anything you would not paste into a public forum

## What can go to cloud

- Open-source repos and public code
- Synthetic or anonymised examples
- Language, framework, and API questions with no data attached
- Architecture and design discussion in the abstract
- This repo itself

## Roles

| Role | Use for | Reached by | State |
|---|---|---|---|
| `local-main` | everything local: code, agent, chat, long context | oMLX `:1234` | live |
| `cloud-cheap` | bulk non-sensitive work, big context | `ask-cloud` → Gemini directly | live |
| `local-embed` | — | — | removed 2026-11-01, nothing called it (ADR 0018) |
| `local-code`, `local-chat` | — | alias names only; mean `local-main` (D1) | collapsed |
| `cloud-code`, `cloud-frontier`, `cloud-frontier-direct`, `cloud-frontier-fast` | — | — | dormant, no keys |

## Enforcement

There is no gateway and no key scope (ADR 0016). The boundary is structural
in a different way:

| Path | Can it leave the host? | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Anything to `127.0.0.1:1234` | **no** | oMLX holds only local models |
| Zed on this host | **no** model egress | no cloud language-model provider (D3). Edit prediction stays `"none"` or laptop `local-edit`; paid Zed Zeta (`"provider": "zed"`) is cloud and trips `zed-sync --check` (ADR 0033). Tool egress: SearXNG in the `web` and `write-web` profiles. Write and Ask have `fetch` and `search_web` off |
| `ask`, `ask-*` except `ask-cloud` | **no** | point at `:1234` with the host token |
| `ask-cloud` | **yes** | the only alias with a provider key; it is explicit by name |
| Sandboxed agents | **no** | egress allowed to `127.0.0.1:1234` only |
| Open WebUI | model: **no**. Search query: **yes if enabled** | see below |

What is lost against the gateway: nothing refuses a cloud request you make
on purpose. Choosing `ask-cloud` on sensitive work is no longer stopped — it
is only made explicit.

**Never add a cloud model to oMLX**, and never give a remote backend an
`omlx` row in `config/roles.tsv`. That single rule is what makes `:1234`
safe for anything.

## Web search punctures the boundary

Open WebUI can give local models web search via self-hosted SearXNG
(`scripts/searxng.sh`). The model, the conversation and your documents all stay
on this machine. **The search query does not.**

SearXNG stops Google, Bing and DuckDuckGo seeing your IP or building a profile —
it queries them on your behalf. That is a genuine improvement over searching
directly. It is not privacy. The search terms still leave.

| Safe with web search on | Never with web search on |
|---|---|
| "How does framework X handle refresh tokens?" | "Search for <person name> <address>" |
| "What changed in the 2026 API spec?" | Anything quoting a name, address, or account figure |
| Framework and API questions | Anything you would not type into Google |

Web search is opt-in per conversation. Leave it off by default. An agent that
can read files can place personal information from those files into the next
query, and that query leaves. A thread that already holds names, addresses,
or documents should not have search on.

Zed follows the same rule on two profiles (ADR 0022, 0029). **Web** sends
`searxng_web_search` to public engines, has no file tools, and stays on
`local-main`. **Write + search** has Write's tools and no pinned model, so
a thread on `local-main` or `local-deep` can search and can read files into
the query. Each tool call asks first (`confirm`). Write and Ask do not
include SearXNG, or Zed's built-in `fetch` and `search_web`. `docs/MCP.md`
uses the former legal-research profile as a setup example; it is not installed.

## No fallbacks

There is no router, so nothing can quietly reroute a request to a cloud
model. If `local-main` is not loaded, a request for it can be answered by
whatever model is loaded, under the name you asked for. That is a wrong
local answer, not a leak. `ask-deep` and the
laptop clients refuse to call `local-deep` unless it is actually loaded, for
this reason. The earlier rule ("local roles never fall back to cloud")
existed because the inherited gateway had `local → gemini` fallbacks; see
`docs/HISTORY.md`.

## Break-glass

There is no second local server (ADR 0028).
If oMLX is wedged: `scripts/omlx.sh down`, then `scripts/omlx.sh up`.
Before changing anything, capture `~/Library/Logs/hearth-omlx.log`, the
footprint of the listener, and the concurrency bench at the failing settings.
# Runbook

Start, stop, diagnose. oMLX serves `127.0.0.1:1234` from `~/.hearth/models`
(ADR 0026). `scripts/omlx.sh down` stops oMLX and leaves the port empty
(ADR 0028).

## Layout of the running system

| Piece | Where | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Server | `127.0.0.1:1234` | oMLX, `scripts/omlx.sh up`. `down` stops it |
| Weights | `~/.hearth/models` | the directories named in `config/roles.tsv` |
| Roles | `config/roles.tsv` | `local-main` resident, 128K × 3. `local-deep` evicts `local-main` for its session. `local-edit` is laptop-only (ADR 0031, 0033) |
| Token | `~/.config/hearth/env`, mode 600 | `OMLX_HOST_TOKEN`. Scripts pass it to the server as `OMLX_API_KEY` |
| Server settings | `~/.omlx/settings.json` (mode 600) | written by `scripts/omlx.sh up`: loopback, CORS off, no model fallback |
| Remote Login (sshd) | `:22` | laptops' tunnel; their keys are `restrict`ed to `-L` `:1234`/`:3000`/`:8888`, with `-R` denied (`scripts/client-setup.sh --update-keys` brings old keys up to date) |
| Open WebUI | Podman, `127.0.0.1:3000` | `scripts/webui.sh` |
| SearXNG | Podman, `:8888` | `scripts/searxng.sh` |

Removed 2026-10-31: Ollama, LiteLLM, Postgres, Rapid-MLX, llmster and their
launchd agents. Ports 4000, 5433 and 11434 should be free.

## First-time setup

1. Put each role's weights at `~/.hearth/models/<weights>` using the path in
   `config/roles.tsv`. Sources are in `docs/MODELS.md`.
2. Store the API key without echoing:
   ```bash
   mkdir -p ~/.config/hearth && umask 077 && \
     printf 'OMLX_HOST_TOKEN=%s\n' "$(pbpaste)" >> ~/.config/hearth/env && \
     chmod 600 ~/.config/hearth/env && pbcopy </dev/null
   ```
   The value is the oMLX API key.
3. Install oMLX either way — Hearth works with both, and does not wipe the
   app's existing `~/.omlx/settings.json`:
   - **Headless:** a venv at `~/.omlx/venv` with `omlx` on that PATH (typical
     early host setup).
   - **DMG:** install `oMLX.app`; it drops `~/.omlx/bin/omlx` → the app CLI.
     Before or after Hearth is fine. `scripts/omlx.sh up` prefers the DMG CLI
     when it runs, falls back to the venv, **merges** host policy into the
     shared settings (loopback, CORS off, `auto_start_on_launch: false`, RAM
     envelope, host token) while leaving UI / aliases / MCP / other app keys
     alone, and picks `local-main`'s `reasoning_parser` from the live
     `/admin/api/grammar/parsers` list (`qwen_3_5` on 0.7.4+, `qwen` on
     0.7.3). If the app is already serving `:1234`, `up` configures that
     process in place (admin API) and does not take it over.
4. `scripts/omlx.sh up` merges settings, links weights as the org/leaf path
   from `roles.tsv` (discovery id = leaf; `model_alias` = role so clients
   still call `local-main`, ADR 0034), serves or attaches, and refuses only
   if a non-oMLX process holds `:1234`.
5. Prove auth holds — a positive control first. Do not `source` the env file
   into a shell you keep; `scripts/healthcheck.sh` reads it itself.
6. `echo "source $PWD/scripts/aliases.zsh" >> ~/.zshrc`

## Surviving a reboot

`scripts/install-autostart.sh --apply` installs `com.hearth.boot`, a launchd
agent that runs `scripts/boot.sh` once at login. `boot.sh` runs
`scripts/omlx.sh up`, then starts the Podman VM, SearXNG and Open WebUI.

The plist sets `AbandonProcessGroup`: without it launchd kills every process
`boot.sh` leaves behind when it exits — which is the Podman VM, so WebUI and
SearXNG died minutes after login (seen and reproduced 2026-10-31).

Log: `~/Library/Logs/hearth-boot.log`. Check it with
`scripts/install-autostart.sh --check`. By hand: `scripts/boot.sh`.

The same install loads `com.hearth.context-watch` (`scripts/context-watch.sh`,
KeepAlive). It watches `~/Library/Logs/hearth-omlx.log` and notifies if a
trim line appears. oMLX does not emit that line today, so it stays quiet.
`scripts/install-autostart.sh --watch` loads only that agent. Notices land in
`~/Library/Logs/hearth-context-watch.log`.

### oMLX

`scripts/boot.sh` runs `scripts/omlx.sh up`. It serves `local-main` from
the copy in `~/.hearth/models` on `127.0.0.1:1234`, with the host token, a
memory guard of RAM minus 8 GB, an 8 GB RAM cache (4 GB below 64 GB RAM),
and a 20 GB SSD cache — or, if oMLX is already listening (app-first install),
merges the same policy into the shared settings and applies it live without
replacing the process. Weights are linked as `~/.omlx/models/<org>/<leaf>`
so omlx.ai benches and the admin UI show the real leaf; `/v1` exposes the
role via `model_alias` (ADR 0034). It does not load `local-edit` (ADR 0033
amend: laptop oMLX only); a leftover pin in `~/.omlx/model_settings.json` is
cleared on `up` so a post-DMG install cannot preload it. `ask-deep` evicts
`local-main` for the session and unloads `local-deep` on the way out
(ADR 0031, 0033). The GPU wired limit is the same RAM minus 8 (57344 MB on
64 GB, 40960 MB on 48 GB), installed by `scripts/iogpu-wired.sh`. Preflight
warns if either differs. `scripts/omlx.sh bench` checks thinking, tools,
and a repeated 30K prompt. `scripts/omlx.sh down` stops a Hearth-started
oMLX (launchd) and leaves the port empty; it does not kill an app-owned
server. A reboot runs `up` again. The Mac app may stay as a settings panel.
A laptop token is registered with `scripts/omlx.sh sub-key add laptop-<name>`
(the prompt does not echo) and dropped with `sub-key revoke`. That key can
call the model and cannot log in as admin. The operator pastes the token.
`sub-key list` prints names.

### Sleep

A sleeping host means every laptop loses inference. These are printed, not
run, because silently changing when your machine sleeps is not something a
script should do:

```bash
sudo pmset -c sleep 0        # never sleep on AC
sudo pmset -c disksleep 0
sudo pmset -a womp 1         # wake on network access
sudo pmset -c displaysleep 10   # displays can still sleep, that is free
```

## Everyday

```bash
ai-status                      # one glance: server, bind, roles, containers, autostart
ai-health                      # scripts/healthcheck.sh — the real verdict (exit 0/1/2)
scripts/preflight.sh           # static checks: roles.tsv, rule 1, oMLX settings, secrets, drift
ai-loaded                      # scripts/omlx.sh status
ai-load                        # scripts/omlx.sh up
ai-restart                     # oMLX down, then up, on 127.0.0.1:1234
ai-log                         # tail -f ~/Library/Logs/hearth-omlx.log
```

Footprint of the model worker (the number that matters against the
GPU ceiling, RAM minus 8 — RSS understates it):

```bash
footprint -p "$(cat ~/.omlx/hearth.pid)" | awk '/Footprint:/'
```

## Chat UI and visibility

```bash
scripts/webui.sh up            # local only — there is no cloud mode, and no login
scripts/webui.sh down
```

The container reaches the host's `127.0.0.1:1234` via
`host.containers.internal` with "serve on local network" off. `webui.sh up`
refuses to start unless the host token gets a 200. SearXNG stays published
on `127.0.0.1:8888` for Zed and laptop tunnels. The WebUI container does not
use that bind: both containers join the `hearth` Podman network and WebUI
calls SearXNG by container name on port 8080. `up` writes that URL into the
WebUI database, which is the value the search toggle actually uses.

**The connection lives in WebUI's database.** Environment variables only seed
a fresh database. `up` does not rewrite the saved OpenAI URL, and the
anonymous session cannot open the admin panel. Default and task model are
`local-main`.

### Using the chat UI from a laptop

Open WebUI is a **web app** — nothing to install on the laptop. **Preferred:
SSH tunnel**, which keeps the UI localhost-only:

```bash
ssh -N -L 3000:127.0.0.1:3000 USER@HOST     # then http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

There is no login: `WEBUI_AUTH` is off, and `webui.sh` refuses `--lan` because
that would publish the chat UI with nothing in front of it. **Do not
port-forward :3000 to the internet.** The tunnel is the only remote path.

The anonymous session does not keep chats. Open WebUI creates that user as
admin, and this image skips temporary-chat enforcement for admins, so
`webui.sh up` demotes the user, turns chat sharing off, deletes saved chats,
and refuses new chat rows. A refresh starts empty. The admin panel is not
available in that session. `up` does not rewrite the saved OpenAI connection;
that row in the WebUI database is what the model list uses.

### Do not enable tools in Open WebUI without thinking

Web search feeds arbitrary attacker-controlled page text straight into the
model's context. That is tolerable **only** because the chat model has no tools:
it can mislead you convincingly, but it cannot act.

Enabling tools, functions, or code execution turns this into the highest-risk
surface in the stack. Open WebUI stores these settings in its database, where
they override the environment and survive container recreation. Treat
enabling them as an ADR-worthy decision. See `docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` §3.

### Web search: the env vars are not enough

Same database-wins trap. The anonymous session cannot open the admin panel,
so the value already stored in the volume is the one the toggle follows.
The toggle then appears per message.

### Vulnerability monitoring (Snyk)

Hearth has no dependency manifests, so what Snyk watches is the two container
images: `scripts/snyk-monitor.sh` exports the image each container is running
(by ID — the tags move) and records it as Snyk projects `hearth/webui` and
`hearth/searxng`, plus the Zed SearXNG MCP server's npm lockfile as
`hearth/mcp-searxng` (ADR 0022). Snyk re-tests those snapshots daily and emails new issues.
Only the image's package list leaves the machine, not this repo's code.

- One-time: `brew tap snyk/tap && brew install snyk-cli`, then `snyk auth`
  (browser sign-in; free plan).
- `scripts/webui.sh up` and `scripts/searxng.sh up` re-run it in the
  background; output in `~/Library/Logs/hearth-snyk.log`. After pulling a new
  image, run `scripts/snyk-monitor.sh` by hand if you did not `up`.
- Exit 2 means it could not run (no CLI, not signed in, Podman down), not that
  the images are clean.

## Running agents contained

```bash
scripts/sandboxed.sh aider              # confine to $PWD
scripts/sandboxed.sh --dir ~/dev/x aider
scripts/sandboxed.sh --print-profile    # audit the profile
scripts/sandbox-probe.sh                # re-verify the controls still hold
```

Inside a sandboxed session the agent can reach **only** `127.0.0.1:1234`.
`pip install`, `npm install`, `git push` and any `curl` fail by design.

**Re-run `sandbox-probe.sh` after every macOS update.** `sandbox-exec` is
deprecated and Apple can change or remove it without notice.

If an agent crashes on a permission error naming a path outside the repo, that
is the sandbox working. Add a preset in `sandboxed.sh` or widen once with
`--also-write PATH`; every widening is printed at startup.

It does **not** protect against an agent reading your keys or corrupting the
repo it was invited into. Commit first; `git diff` before accepting.

## Diagnosing

| Symptom | Check | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Every client 401s | `curl` with the host token | token wrong or missing in `~/.config/hearth/env`; Zed keeps its own copy in the keychain |
| Wrong model answers | `scripts/healthcheck.sh` | An unknown model name can be answered by the loaded role. Check the client's model id is a role |
| Slow under load | `concurrency-bench.sh` at the failing settings | prefill is serialised; see `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` |
| Zed agent stalls | `ai-log`, `scripts/status.sh`, bench | capture them **before** changing anything |
| Server LAN-visible or CORS on | `ai-status`, `ai-health` | `scripts/boot.sh` restarts it on 127.0.0.1 without CORS |
| aider errors naming `ollama_chat/…` or `local-code` | an old `.aider.conf.yml` in the repo dir | roles are `openai/local-main` now |

Bench any change the same way as the baseline:

```bash
scripts/concurrency-bench.sh --base-url http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1 \
  --token-env OMLX_HOST_TOKEN --model local-main --concurrency 3
```

## Rollback

There is no old stack to roll back to: it was removed on 2026-10-31 (ADR
0016, Rollback). Backups are in `~/hearth-backups/` (mode 700). Client config
backups from 2026-10-31 sit beside the live files with a dated suffix.

## Laptop access

SSH tunnel only; the server stays on `127.0.0.1`. `docs/LAPTOP.md` has the
whole setup. On the host:

```bash
scripts/client-setup.sh <laptop-name>                       # print the steps
scripts/client-setup.sh <laptop-name> --authorize key.pub   # tunnel-only key
```

`healthcheck.sh` section 5 should say "LAN: no answer" — a LAN answer means
the server is not bound to loopback. Revoke a laptop with
`scripts/omlx.sh sub-key revoke laptop-<name>` and delete its
`hearth-tunnel-<name>` line in `~/.ssh/authorized_keys`.
**Never port-forward `:22`, `:1234` or `:3000`.**
# hearth — what it solves, what it measured, what's next

> **Historical (gateway era).** Describes the LiteLLM + Ollama stack; superseded by ADR 0016.

Written 2026-08-02. All figures below are measured on the machine, not estimated.

---

## 1. The problems this actually solves

### Privacy that survives forgetting

The real risk was never "I might paste client data into ChatGPT." It was that
in a hurry, on a deadline, the fast option and the safe option are different
buttons — and eventually the wrong one gets pressed.

So the boundary is **structural, not advisory**. Client work uses a `private`
API key whose model list contains no cloud models. Ask it for Gemini and the
gateway returns 403. There is no prompt that talks its way around a key scope.

The inherited config had a fallback rule sending `local → gemini` on any Ollama
timeout. Every slow local response was silently shipping the prompt to Google.
Nothing in the setup would ever have told me. **Across a privacy boundary a
fallback is a leak, not resilience** — `audit-fallbacks.sh` now fails the build
if that pattern reappears.

### One place to change models instead of four

Before: aider pointed at Ollama, Cursor at cloud providers, scripts elsewhere.
Changing model meant editing four things and missing one.

Now everything asks for a **role** — `local-chat`, `local-code`, `local-big`,
`local-embed`, `cloud-cheap`. Concrete model names live in exactly two files.
Swapping a model is one config edit, no client changes. Given how fast this
field moves, that indirection is the single most valuable design choice here.

### Knowing where requests went, and what they cost

`usage.sh --summary` gives the LOCAL vs CLOUD token split — the proportion of
work that never left the machine. Virtual keys carry spend caps that act as
fuses: a leaked key or a runaway overnight loop hits a wall instead of a card.

### Containment for agents (added today)

Coding agents run with full user permissions and read whatever you point them
at. The gateway governs where data goes *on purpose*; it has no jurisdiction
over a compromised agent, which would just call `curl` directly and never touch
LiteLLM at all.

Current state of the field, 2026: prompt injection **cannot be reliably
detected** — adaptive attacks bypass essentially every published defence, and
the best of them still miss around one in ten. OWASP has it as the leading
agentic-AI failure in production, with coding agents driving most of the volume.

So there is nothing to filter. The only thing that works is cutting capability.
`scripts/sandboxed.sh` runs an agent with egress denied to everything except
the gateway. It read a secret? Fine — there's no channel to send it down.

Verified on macOS 26.4, 10 checks passed. It proved itself within seconds of
first use: aider's startup call to pypi.org was refused by the sandbox.

**Deliberately not solved:** reads stay unrestricted (restricting them breaks
every language runtime), and an agent can still corrupt the repo it was invited
into. That second one is handled by committing before a session and reading
`git diff` after — a workflow habit, not a control.

---

## 2. Routerly comparison

[github.com/Inebrio/Routerly](https://github.com/Inebrio/Routerly), AGPL-3.0.
It targets the exact thing that is most annoying about the current setup.

| | Routerly | LiteLLM (what I run) |
|---|---|---|
| Database | **none** — JSON files | Postgres + Prisma, 64 tables |
| Native Anthropic `/v1/messages` | **yes** | no — OpenAI shape only |
| Per-project tokens + budgets | yes | yes (virtual keys) |
| Routing policies | 9, incl. LLM-picks-the-model | static fallbacks |
| Providers | 8 | 100+ |
| Maturity | v0.1.5 · 43 stars · 3 contributors | widely deployed |

**The genuine unlock is native Anthropic format.** Claude Code is currently a
documented exception in my stack because LiteLLM only speaks OpenAI shape.
Routerly handles `/v1/messages` natively, so Claude Code could route through
the gateway and pick up spend tracking and logging. That is a real capability
gain, not a tidiness argument.

**Why I am not switching:**

- v0.1.5, one release, three contributors. This is pre-1.0 software that would
  hold my provider keys and enforce my privacy boundary. LiteLLM's 64 tables
  are ugly; **ugly is not the same as risky.**
- The cost of switching is the *verification*, not the config. Leak test,
  fallback audit, digest pinning, every script touching `/key/*` — all would
  need redoing and re-proving. That is the expensive part, and it buys removing
  a container I never look at.
- AGPL plus a commercial-licensing contact is an open-core shape. Worth
  watching whether the free tier narrows.

Revisit at 1.0, or the first time Postgres causes an actual problem rather than
merely offending my sense of proportion.

---

## 3. Token speed on a 64 GB Mac — the counter-intuitive part

### Throughput is not the number that matters

Benched three models across four fixed tasks, through the gateway:

| Model | tokens emitted | wall clock | avg tok/s |
|---|---|---|---|
| `qwen3-coder:30b` | 1,218 | 11.2 s | 108.8 |
| `gpt-oss:20b` | 4,466 (3.7x) | 47.6 s | 93.8 |
| `qwen3:8b` (thinking on) | 13,840 (11.4x) | 181.4 s | 76.3 |

Throughput barely moves — **65–120 tok/s across everything**, because the
machine is memory-bandwidth bound. The 8B model is not meaningfully faster per
token than the 30B one.

**Wall clock is set by how many tokens a model emits, not how fast it emits
them.** The smallest model was the slowest overall, by 16x, because it is a
hybrid reasoning model that thinks out loud by default. On one task it spent
**100 seconds and 7,548 tokens** where the coding model took 1.6 s and 192
tokens — forty times the output for the same question.

Fix: Ollama's `think: false` parameter. `local-chat` went from **2,370 tokens /
29.7 s to 386 tokens / 5.1 s** through the gateway. From slowest role to
fastest.

Worth noting I got this wrong twice and measurement corrected me both times. I
doubted `think: false` would work — it did. I was confident `/no_think` in the
prompt would work — it was silently ignored, 2,236 tokens before and 2,261
after.

### Residency beats model quality

The old `local-big` was llama3.3:70b at 42 GB. Cold, through the gateway:

| Role | Model | Time to first reply |
|---|---|---|
| `local-code` | qwen2.5-coder:32b | 29 s |
| `local-big` | **llama3.3:70b** | **440 s** |
| `cloud-cheap` | gemini-3.5-flash | 2 s |

Seven and a half minutes. Not because the model is slow — because at 42 GB it
could not stay resident alongside anything else, so every switch meant
reloading 42 GB from disk. Replaced with `gpt-oss:20b`: **440 s → 5 s.** Not a
faster model, a resident one.

### The real memory ceiling is not 64 GB

macOS caps GPU-accessible unified memory at ~75% above 36 GB — so **~48 GB on a
64 GB machine**, not 64. I had ~54 GB written in the docs and it was wrong.

| | Old set | Current set |
|---|---|---|
| Weights resident | 65.7 GB (impossible) | **37.5 GB** |
| Against ceiling | ~48 GB | ~48 GB |
| Disk | 63 GB | ~37 GB |

Current: `qwen3:8b` 5.2 GB + `qwen3-coder:30b` 19 GB + `gpt-oss:20b` 13 GB +
`nomic-embed-text` 0.3 GB. All four stay loaded; switching roles evicts nothing.

**MoE models are what make this work.** `qwen3-coder:30b` is 30B parameters but
activates ~3B per token, so it runs at roughly 7B speed while fitting in 19 GB.

### On switching to llama-server

Looked at it after seeing Vitalik Buterin's writeup. **Expected saving: about
zero.** His gain came from a hard VRAM wall on a discrete 24 GB GPU, where a
model either fits or collapses to CPU offload. Unified memory has no such
cliff, and Ollama on Apple Silicon now runs on MLX — Apple's own framework for
this architecture. Cost would be ~15 scripts and ~60 call sites, to probably go
slower. Not doing it.

---

## 4. What's left

1. **Audit untrusted MCP servers** — the one remaining unknown. Fetches from the
   open web, sits next to real client data, nobody has asked what tools it hands
   a model.
2. **Re-bench with thinking off** and actually *read* the task 03 output.
   `gpt-oss:20b` earned `local-big` on capacity, not quality — nothing has yet
   confirmed it reasons well. Task 03 hides a float-for-currency bug in
   money arithmetic. A syntax check cannot answer that; it needs a human.
3. **Record model digests** from a verified state, so drift is detectable.
4. **Local reference corpus** via `local-embed`, so the queries most worth
   not leaking never reach a search engine at all.
5. **Confirmation gates** — costs nothing today because nothing in the stack can
   send mail or move money. Matters the day something can.

### Sharing the repo

Close. No secrets in any tracked file, and no hardcoded home paths in
executable code — 68 uses of `$HOME` instead. What remains is naming: a LAN IP
as a default in the client scripts, a personal launchd label, business-specific
rules in the agent instructions, and no LICENSE. Roughly half a day.

One item there is a real bug rather than cosmetic: `healthcheck.sh` probes a
hardcoded LAN address to confirm Ollama is *not* reachable. On any other
machine that address is unreachable anyway, so the check passes and reports the
hole closed on a box where it may be wide open. Same false-pass shape as the
leak test bug — unreachable and secure produce identical output.

---

## The pattern worth keeping

Nearly every real problem here was found by measurement, not reasoning:

- The fallback leak — found by reading config, not by it failing.
- `model_info: tier: local` — a valid-looking field that made LiteLLM silently
  drop **every model** while still passing its own readiness check.
- The leak test that passed against a dormant model, where "model not found"
  also returns 4xx. A broken key and a perfectly scoped key looked identical.
- The 70B — retired on a 440-second measurement, not on an opinion about 70B
  models.
- `/no_think` — confidently predicted to work, silently ignored.

The recurring shape: **a check that cannot run must never look like a check
that passed.** Preflight now separates ERROR (couldn't run) from FAIL (real
problem), and every security test pairs a positive control with the negative
one. That single distinction has caught more genuine problems than any
individual feature in the stack.
# Prompt injection threat model

Last updated: 2026-11-05. Controls in §4 verified on macOS 26.4 by
`scripts/sandbox-probe.sh` (10 passed, 0 failed). Write + search (ADR 0029)
is listed in §2 and §3; it is outside that sandbox.

## 1. The claim this document rests on

**Prompt injection cannot be detected reliably, and we do not try.**

As of 2026 the strongest published defences still miss roughly one in ten
optimisation-based attacks, and adaptive attacks bypass essentially every
recommended defence. OWASP's 2026 data has prompt injection as the leading
agentic-AI security failure in production, with coding agents driving most of
the new attack volume — **aider is named among the five agents responsible**,
and it is the one used daily here.

The consequence for design: there is no filter to install, no delimiter scheme
to adopt, and no classifier worth running. The only defences that hold are
**capability restrictions** — reducing what an agent is able to do, so that a
successful injection has nowhere to go.

Anything in this repo that looks like injection *detection* is a bug.

## 2. Why the existing privacy boundary does not help

This is the important structural point, and it is easy to get wrong.

Model placement is the control for **where data goes on purpose**. oMLX
on `:1234` holds only local models, and only `ask-cloud` carries a cloud key, so
a prompt sent to a local role cannot route personal information to Google. That boundary
is real and it works (ADR 0016; before that it was gateway key scope).

Injection attacks do not use that path at all.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph inputs["Untrusted inputs into the agent"]
        web["web pages via SearXNG"]
        repo["repo files / vendored deps"]
        mcpsrc["untrusted MCP - UNAUDITED"]
    end

    subgraph sandbox["sandbox-exec - one egress allowed"]
        agent["aider / Zed agent"]
        wd["working dir - writable"]
    end

    secrets["~/.ssh, .env, keychain<br/>readable - accepted"]

    gw{{"oMLX 127.0.0.1:1234<br/>local models only, token required"}}
    localm["local models"]
    cloudm["cloud models"]
    outside["blocked: github / pypi / attacker"]

    web --> agent
    repo --> agent
    mcpsrc --> agent
    secrets -.-> agent

    agent -->|"only permitted egress"| gw
    agent --> wd
    agent -.->|"blocked by sandbox"| outside

    gw --> localm
    cloudm -.-|"not served by oMLX"| gw

    classDef okCls fill:#E1F5EE,stroke:#0F6E56,color:#085041
    classDef badCls fill:#FBE9E7,stroke:#A32B18,color:#7A1F11
    classDef warnCls fill:#FAEEDA,stroke:#854F0B,color:#633806
    classDef boxCls fill:#EEEDFE,stroke:#534AB7,color:#3C3489

    class gw,localm okCls
    class outside badCls
    class secrets,mcpsrc warnCls
    class agent,wd boxCls
```

Two boundaries, doing different jobs:

- **`sandbox-exec`** wraps the agent. One arrow leaves it, to `127.0.0.1:1234`.
  Everything else — `github`, `pypi`, an attacker's host, DNS itself — is cut.
- **oMLX** is the only thing at the end of that arrow, and it holds only
  local models, so even intended traffic cannot reach a cloud model.

An injection that lands still reads whatever it likes (the dashed arrow from
secrets is deliberate) and can still write into the working directory. What it
cannot do is get anything out.

An injected agent that reads `~/.ssh/id_rsa` and POSTs it to an attacker
**never touches the model server**. Which models are loaded has no bearing on
that path.

So: the privacy boundary governs *intentional* routing. The containment
boundary in §4 governs *everything else*. They are different axes and neither
substitutes for the other.

| Threat | Model placement | Sandbox |
|---|---|---|
| A prompt sends personal information to a cloud model | **caught** (unless you run `ask-cloud`) | not caught |
| An injected agent exfiltrates a key via curl | not caught | **caught**, if the agent was started with `scripts/sandboxed.sh` |
| Write + search puts file text into a SearXNG query | not caught | not caught. Zed's 1.14 sandbox covers its terminal and fetch tools, not MCP. `confirm` shows the query before it leaves |
| An injected agent reads a file it shouldn't | not caught | not caught |
| An injected agent rewrites source in the repo | not caught | not caught |

Note the bottom two rows. They are not oversights; see §5.

## 3. Surfaces, ranked by capability rather than likelihood

Ranking by likelihood invites guessing. Rank by *what an injection could do if
it landed*, which is knowable.

| # | Surface | Untrusted content arrives via | If injected, it can | Risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **aider** | files you `/add`, vendored deps, cloned repos, sample data | edit source, run commands, reach the network | **high** |
| 2 | **Zed agents** | same, plus tool results. Write and Ask have `fetch` and `search_web` off. **Web** is search only. **Write + search** has Write's tools and `searxng_web_search` (ADR 0029) | edit or read in Write; send the query out from Web or Write + search. Write + search can put file text into that query. `confirm` is the gate | **high** |
| 3 | **untrusted MCP** | fetched docs / tools from a server not in `docs/MCP.md` | depends entirely on what the MCP exposes | unknown |
| 4 | **SearXNG** | arbitrary web pages; the query text leaves | Open WebUI can mislead you and, when search is on, send a query. Zed's `web` profile can search and cannot read files. **Write + search** can read files and search in one thread. The tunnel publishes SearXNG with no login, so any process on a laptop can send a query | medium |

**Surface 3 is a server that is not registered in `docs/MCP.md`.** The live
web-search MCP server is registered there, with the tool it exposes written
down. The legal-research server is a worked setup example, not an installed
server. Anything else is still unknown.

**Surface 4.** Open WebUI's search is opt-in per message. The Zed `web`
profile can search and cannot read files. **Write + search** can do both,
so a file the agent reads can leave as the query. `confirm` on each tool
call is the gate: the query text is on screen before it is sent. SearXNG
does not keep a log of successful queries. `podman logs hearth-searxng`
shows a query only when an upstream engine logs an error. **The moment Open WebUI gains tools, functions, or code
execution, that UI becomes surface 1 with a web feed attached.** Treat
enabling them as a decision requiring an ADR, not a settings toggle.

## 4. What is actually enforced

`scripts/sandboxed.sh` runs an agent under a `sandbox-exec` profile. Verified
on macOS 26.4, 2026-08-02:

| Control | State |
|---|---|
| Egress to anything but `127.0.0.1:1234` | **blocked** — verified against 1.1.1.1 and the old `:4000` (2026-10-31) |
| DNS resolution | **blocked** — hostnames do not resolve |
| Writes outside the working directory | **blocked** — verified against `$HOME` |
| Writes inside the working directory | allowed |
| Reads | **unrestricted, deliberately** |

Usage:

```bash
scripts/sandboxed.sh aider                 # confine to $PWD
scripts/sandboxed.sh --dir ~/dev/x aider   # confine to a named repo
scripts/sandboxed.sh --print-profile       # audit the profile, any machine
scripts/sandbox-probe.sh                   # re-verify the controls hold
```

Re-run the probe after any macOS update. `sandbox-exec` is deprecated and Apple
could remove or change it without notice; the probe is how you find out that
happened deliberately rather than by being breached.

### Why reads are open

Restricting reads breaks dylib loading and every language runtime, and read
confinement is not what stops the attack. An agent under this profile **can
still read your SSH keys, `.env` files, and browser cookies.** It cannot send
them anywhere. Inside this profile, exfiltration needs a channel, and there
is exactly one open channel — oMLX on `:1234`, which holds only local models.
Zed is not started under this profile. Write + search is a second channel,
and this sandbox does not cover it.

This is a deliberate trade, not a limitation we failed to notice. Anyone
reading this document should be able to state it back: *the sandbox protects
against exfiltration, not against reading.*

### Why the working directory is writable

An agent that cannot write is not an agent. The residual risk is that an
injected agent **corrupts the repo it was invited into** — plausible, and not
mitigated by the sandbox. It is mitigated by git: commit before starting an
agent session, and `git diff` before accepting anything. That is a workflow
control and it depends on you, which is why it is written down here rather than
assumed.

### What the sandbox is not

`sandbox-exec` is deprecated, has known weaknesses, and this profile allows
broad `mach-lookup` and `process-exec` because nothing runs without them. A
determined attacker with code execution may find leverage there.

This is **not** a VM and not a security boundary of that grade. It raises the
cost of an attack and closes the one channel that turns a compromise into a
loss. Do not extend trust to it beyond that. If the threat model ever includes
a motivated attacker specifically targeting this machine, the answer is a Lima
VM or a separate user account, not a better SBPL profile.

## 5. Accepted, unmitigated risk

Recorded so nobody mistakes silence for safety.

1. **An injected agent can read any file the user can.** Mitigated only by the
   absence of an egress channel. If a future change opens egress — even to a
   package registry — this becomes live. That is why `--also-write` and any
   network widening need justification.
2. **An injected agent can corrupt its own repo.** Mitigated by git hygiene,
   not by the sandbox.
3. **Untrusted MCP servers are unaudited.** Unknown capability, real-data adjacency.
4. **Zed's sandbox has holes by design.** Since 1.14 it covers only the
   agent's terminal and fetch tools. Each host in
   `agent.sandbox_permissions.network_hosts` is an egress path, and ACP
   agents (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI) bypass profiles, `confirm` and the
   sandbox entirely, calling their own cloud. None on the host
   (`docs/IDES.md`).
5. **No confirmation gates** beyond git commits. This costs nothing today
   because nothing in the stack can send mail or move money. It becomes urgent
   the day something can.

## 6. Cheapest next checks

In order of value per effort:

1. Audit what tools each MCP exposes to a model. Surface 3 is the only
   unknown on the list.
2. Run aider under the sandbox for a week of real work and record what it
   needed widened. A profile nobody can work under gets switched off.
3. Confirm Open WebUI still has tools disabled after any upgrade — its settings
   live in a database that overrides the environment, a trap already documented
   in `RUNBOOK.md`.
4. Known vulnerabilities in the Open WebUI and SearXNG images are monitored by
   Snyk (`scripts/snyk-monitor.sh`, RUNBOOK). That covers published CVEs in
   their packages, not their configuration or oMLX itself.

